WEBVTT

00:05.220 --> 00:07.680
- [Louis] There's so much kinda
of trans stuff going on now,

00:07.680 --> 00:09.940
but it tends to all be white, you know?

00:09.940 --> 00:12.970
and I don't really wanna
kind of support the idea

00:12.970 --> 00:15.950
that all trans folks, or all transsexuals

00:15.950 --> 00:17.580
are all European-Americans.

00:17.580 --> 00:21.010
- [Kylar] You know, we have gay TV now.

00:21.010 --> 00:23.580
I was just watching it the other day

00:23.580 --> 00:25.291
and they have tons of trans people,

00:25.291 --> 00:27.490
it's like, but none of color, any color.

00:27.490 --> 00:29.877
So I just think it's important
that we get our stories told

00:29.877 --> 00:31.410
and our voices heard.

00:31.410 --> 00:33.528
- [Nicholas] I hope that this film

00:33.528 --> 00:36.110
just really just get out there to where

00:36.110 --> 00:38.979
it wouldn't be a question
like, or someone said,

00:38.979 --> 00:42.146
"I never heard of a trans guy before."

00:43.420 --> 00:45.617
To where it'll just
be, "Oh yeah, you know,

00:45.617 --> 00:47.753
"he a T guy or whatever,"

00:47.753 --> 00:50.160
and put it on top that he's Black

00:50.160 --> 00:51.530
You know.

00:51.530 --> 00:52.623
It's like, wow.

00:53.980 --> 00:56.480
(jazzy music)

01:02.800 --> 01:07.110
- As a kid, I always
used to play Perry Mason.

01:07.110 --> 01:08.340
I was weird kid, you know,

01:08.340 --> 01:10.410
other people wanted to
play dolls or whatever.

01:10.410 --> 01:11.920
I wanted to play Perry Mason.

01:11.920 --> 01:14.638
So I would, and every Saturday,

01:14.638 --> 01:17.330
after watching the
episodes of Perry Mason,

01:17.330 --> 01:20.630
my bed, and my room
would be my little table

01:20.630 --> 01:23.490
and I would, you know, do cases.

01:23.490 --> 01:27.830
And so, and I mean, I probably
was four or five then.

01:27.830 --> 01:30.803
So, and then I did that and
I really just loved that.

01:32.380 --> 01:37.140
And then as I grew, this
activism part of me grew,

01:37.140 --> 01:39.130
but it was because my parents

01:39.130 --> 01:41.660
were very active in the community.

01:41.660 --> 01:45.310
For them, their activism was
through the American Legion,

01:45.310 --> 01:49.094
because my parents, my father
was a World War II veteran,

01:49.094 --> 01:52.300
my mother 10 years younger than he,

01:52.300 --> 01:54.700
and they've been lifelong sweethearts.

01:54.700 --> 01:59.700
And since she was 14, through
their whole lives together,

02:00.750 --> 02:02.840
so, but they were very
active in the community,

02:02.840 --> 02:05.340
through the American
Legion, through the church,

02:05.340 --> 02:07.623
you know, Second Baptist Church.

02:08.620 --> 02:12.200
And so I saw that and went with them

02:12.200 --> 02:15.990
to clean the graveyards, to
mow other people's lawns,

02:15.990 --> 02:20.220
to do these things, and
also just their attitudes

02:20.220 --> 02:22.980
of you stand up for what you believe in.

02:22.980 --> 02:25.220
And both my parents were stand up

02:25.220 --> 02:26.913
for what you believe in people,

02:27.980 --> 02:31.200
that, that just propelled my advocacy.

02:31.200 --> 02:36.200
And when my life unfolded,
it was so important to fight

02:37.070 --> 02:40.360
for rights of just
human beings in general.

02:40.360 --> 02:43.460
And, and of course that
is my love and my passion.

02:43.460 --> 02:46.530
I worked in corporate
America and went to work

02:46.530 --> 02:48.380
in corporate America after law school,

02:48.380 --> 02:50.670
again, thinking I'm not
going to be the cliche,

02:50.670 --> 02:53.310
the black civil rights lawyer,

02:53.310 --> 02:55.360
the black lesbian at the time,

02:55.360 --> 02:57.730
'cause that was the title that I thought

02:57.730 --> 03:00.223
was closest to who I was at the time.

03:02.352 --> 03:04.070
And so, I went to work
in this corporation,

03:04.070 --> 03:05.300
gonna make lots of money.

03:05.300 --> 03:07.240
So I was going to conform.

03:07.240 --> 03:09.898
It was horrible because
then I had to dress

03:09.898 --> 03:14.898
or try to dress like they
wanted with hose and makeup.

03:15.460 --> 03:18.400
And I don't know any people
that wear those things,

03:18.400 --> 03:21.140
but it's quite costly
to buy makeup and hose

03:21.140 --> 03:22.640
and all those things, and
then you have to get up

03:22.640 --> 03:25.325
at the crack of dawn to get dressed.

03:25.325 --> 03:26.158
(audience laughing)

03:26.158 --> 03:28.150
So you can tell, I'm kinda poor at that,

03:28.150 --> 03:29.620
which is why my head is shaved.

03:29.620 --> 03:32.397
I've never been for getting up early, but,

03:32.397 --> 03:35.350
(audience laughing)
but at any rate.

03:35.350 --> 03:37.180
I would just rip those clothes off,

03:37.180 --> 03:39.810
as soon as I got in the
car, out of the work,

03:39.810 --> 03:42.440
in the parking lot, you
know, and I couldn't stand,

03:42.440 --> 03:44.940
you know, just as soon
as I could get them off.

03:44.940 --> 03:47.260
I mean, I'd just be undressing as soon

03:47.260 --> 03:49.640
as I got to the parking lot,
'cause it was just horrible

03:49.640 --> 03:52.610
and the makeup, the thought of makeup,

03:52.610 --> 03:56.960
and then I tried to use
as little as possible.

03:56.960 --> 03:59.370
And luckily, my skin is sensitive,

03:59.370 --> 04:01.500
so I could use that as an excuse

04:01.500 --> 04:05.020
to not wear makeup or what they expected.

04:05.020 --> 04:07.150
And those dress codes held through,

04:07.150 --> 04:09.450
really for most women into the '90s.

04:09.450 --> 04:11.970
And in some places, depending
upon where you live,

04:11.970 --> 04:13.650
that's still a fight.

04:13.650 --> 04:17.780
And so my girlfriend, every
day we'd get up and say,

04:17.780 --> 04:21.120
okay, this is a uniform, to
get me dressed every day.

04:21.120 --> 04:23.757
I mean, it was really a
psychological, it's like,

04:23.757 --> 04:26.190
okay, okay, and I mean,
I would put the stuff on

04:26.190 --> 04:29.874
and I would be so uncomfortable
carrying the purse around.

04:29.874 --> 04:31.420
I'll never forget that.

04:31.420 --> 04:33.420
I had a good buddy that I worked with,

04:33.420 --> 04:35.380
and he would remember the purse

04:35.380 --> 04:37.190
because I had never remembered the purse.

04:37.190 --> 04:39.850
And he's like, God, you carry
that like a lunch bucket

04:39.850 --> 04:41.390
and blah, blah, blah, blah.

04:41.390 --> 04:43.960
And I'm like, dude, you know, whatever.

04:43.960 --> 04:45.750
So I gave up the purse part,

04:45.750 --> 04:49.787
but he said to me, when I
stopped trying to do that,

04:49.787 --> 04:52.277
"Thank God you stopped doing that.

04:52.277 --> 04:53.840
"You looked horrible."

04:53.840 --> 04:56.500
And I said to him, "You're my,"

04:56.500 --> 04:59.037
at the time, "very good friend.

04:59.037 --> 05:00.587
"Why didn't you tell me?

05:00.587 --> 05:05.110
"I expect my friends to tell
me the truth," and he says,

05:05.110 --> 05:07.210
you know, he didn't want
to hurt my feelings.

05:07.210 --> 05:09.330
It's just like, well, next
time, tell me the truth.

05:09.330 --> 05:10.920
And I did, I looked horrible.

05:10.920 --> 05:12.050
And I liked to say, I looked

05:12.050 --> 05:15.380
like a bad drag queen, and I did, so.

05:15.380 --> 05:18.490
And I don't think I was fooling anybody

05:18.490 --> 05:23.490
but myself because who I
was transcended the clothes.

05:25.268 --> 05:29.960
And eventually I got a new
boss who'd made my life hell

05:29.960 --> 05:32.490
and I was outta there
after announcing transition

05:32.490 --> 05:36.260
in six months, six months,
it was like fast, boom.

05:36.260 --> 05:37.820
And I had been a good employee.

05:37.820 --> 05:40.660
I just didn't think that
company would ever do that.

05:40.660 --> 05:43.370
Just Pollyanna, I did a good job.

05:43.370 --> 05:45.710
Why would they wanna get rid of me?

05:45.710 --> 05:50.110
Because we know that how you get promoted

05:50.110 --> 05:52.830
is how you look, right?

05:52.830 --> 05:56.050
How we get elected or how we
do anything is how we look.

05:56.050 --> 05:58.340
So if we're treated differently,

05:58.340 --> 06:01.920
and I can always analogize
this part of me to race,

06:01.920 --> 06:04.320
where a lot of times
we're treated differently

06:04.320 --> 06:07.570
because we were a certain race,
that really ostracizes us,

06:07.570 --> 06:09.530
it doesn't really help or benefit us

06:09.530 --> 06:14.023
in any realm of the imagination
and makes matters worse.

06:15.977 --> 06:20.240
That's the whole dress thing
that I got on at Harvard,

06:20.240 --> 06:22.600
because I'm really passionate about that,

06:22.600 --> 06:25.250
about people feeling
like they can go to work

06:25.250 --> 06:28.130
and be comfortable, and
not have an expectation

06:28.130 --> 06:32.050
of having to be a certain
way or to be forced

06:32.050 --> 06:36.970
to be feminized or, or
gendered or whatever.

06:36.970 --> 06:39.470
I started doing a lot of trans conferences

06:39.470 --> 06:42.480
because I wanted to get legal information

06:42.480 --> 06:44.663
out there about name changes,

06:45.650 --> 06:47.880
document changes, and so forth.

06:47.880 --> 06:50.130
And people weren't doing that then.

06:50.130 --> 06:52.740
And the big organization,
nobody was doing that then.

06:52.740 --> 06:55.020
And me, and I would
meet a couple other guys

06:55.020 --> 06:56.960
doing the trans circuit, so to speak,

06:56.960 --> 06:59.120
and we'd go to conferences and doing that.

06:59.120 --> 07:02.360
And that kinda started my
advocacy in the trans community,

07:02.360 --> 07:06.100
just doing those Trans 101s
of how you get your documents,

07:06.100 --> 07:07.370
where you get your documents,

07:07.370 --> 07:10.160
and how to navigate the process.

07:10.160 --> 07:13.300
And that helped me come into, you know,

07:13.300 --> 07:14.800
because then that allowed me to connect

07:14.800 --> 07:18.490
to other trans people
and then to learn more

07:18.490 --> 07:20.440
about myself and embrace myself.

07:20.440 --> 07:22.657
And one of the best things
were going to the conferences,

07:22.657 --> 07:24.530
And I remember True Spirit Conference

07:24.530 --> 07:26.480
when it was just like a few of us,

07:26.480 --> 07:29.050
then a grew to larger and larger

07:29.050 --> 07:32.661
and a lot more trans people,
but just being able to go there

07:32.661 --> 07:36.150
and have somebody understand, you know,

07:36.150 --> 07:39.295
even without saying
what your experience is

07:39.295 --> 07:43.210
and just going, wow, look at
you, look at you, look at you.

07:43.210 --> 07:45.540
You know, it's like,
wow, you're buff there,

07:45.540 --> 07:46.970
you're this you're that, you know,

07:46.970 --> 07:48.670
all these things are happening.

07:48.670 --> 07:52.660
What are you doing about
scars on your chest?

07:52.660 --> 07:55.570
You know, is my surgery comparable?

07:55.570 --> 07:57.780
Not meaning, you know, we're one ups man,

07:57.780 --> 08:00.050
but did I get a good job or not?

08:00.050 --> 08:03.320
Does it look real and
showing each other our chest,

08:03.320 --> 08:07.486
you know, talking about
issues, how do you date?

08:07.486 --> 08:09.960
You know, how do you dress?

08:09.960 --> 08:13.550
And then it was nice
doing ones with the MTFs,

08:13.550 --> 08:16.550
because they would like
exchange clothes with us

08:16.550 --> 08:20.710
to help with teaching
guys how to tie ties.

08:20.710 --> 08:24.410
And I think that was really a
part of my growing experience

08:24.410 --> 08:27.430
is going to the trans
conferences and connecting

08:27.430 --> 08:30.230
with other people, and
now they've grown up

08:30.230 --> 08:33.470
to such much, more advocacy,

08:33.470 --> 08:36.290
much more politically astute conferences,

08:36.290 --> 08:37.740
which is a good thing.

08:37.740 --> 08:41.917
But the beginning ones, when
it was just a few people

08:41.917 --> 08:45.070
coming together, were
some of the best ones.

08:45.070 --> 08:46.880
When I first started my activism,

08:46.880 --> 08:50.930
I talked a lot about race and just being,

08:50.930 --> 08:54.120
you know, gay, lesbian,
bisexual, transgender.

08:54.120 --> 08:55.850
And when, particularly, when I would go

08:55.850 --> 08:59.430
to transgender conferences,
'cause I didn't want us to do

08:59.430 --> 09:03.680
what the gay movement had done,
but we do, we're doing it,

09:03.680 --> 09:06.570
exactly the same thing,
'cause we are part of that

09:06.570 --> 09:09.510
and have become part of
that whole hierarchy.

09:09.510 --> 09:13.090
And you see it in the
movement, they will hire,

09:13.090 --> 09:15.780
and I got in trouble with saying
this in corporate America,

09:15.780 --> 09:18.240
but they will hire a white person

09:18.240 --> 09:22.160
over a qualified black person
and think nothing of it.

09:22.160 --> 09:25.170
And they're, well, where can
we find qualified black people?

09:25.170 --> 09:26.150
Where are they at?

09:26.150 --> 09:29.520
Where are qualified Hispanic
people, Asian people,

09:29.520 --> 09:32.100
when we're there, we're
there at the table,

09:32.100 --> 09:34.730
we're visible, but we're looked over.

09:34.730 --> 09:37.350
We're never considered as qualified.

09:37.350 --> 09:41.370
And then we don't get into
the decision making positions

09:41.370 --> 09:44.230
in the organizations that make decisions

09:44.230 --> 09:48.020
about the gay movement and the decisions

09:48.020 --> 09:51.560
that are made in lobbying
for bills and legislation.

09:51.560 --> 09:53.440
Sure, we get invited to the table,

09:53.440 --> 09:56.120
one or two of us get invited to the table

09:56.120 --> 09:58.400
because a few people feel like, oh,

09:58.400 --> 09:59.860
well, we should look for parity,

09:59.860 --> 10:03.350
but I don't feel like that's
a real concern in the,

10:03.350 --> 10:06.843
at large movement, if
you will, that it is,

10:06.843 --> 10:11.809
be diverse racially,
economically or otherwise,

10:11.809 --> 10:15.340
because no oppression
is greater than another.

10:15.340 --> 10:18.884
And I get very angry about
that when I have some people,

10:18.884 --> 10:21.270
particularly people that are not of color,

10:21.270 --> 10:25.140
try to call me on that because
there is a lot of energy

10:25.140 --> 10:28.850
and anger around being
of color in this country.

10:28.850 --> 10:30.410
And we have a right to own that.

10:30.410 --> 10:33.770
And I had somebody say, oh,
well, one's not any greater,

10:33.770 --> 10:35.800
well, no one isn't any
greater than the other,

10:35.800 --> 10:39.390
but don't assume, you know my experience

10:39.390 --> 10:41.440
until you've walked in my shoes

10:43.641 --> 10:47.580
and don't dismiss it at all.

10:47.580 --> 10:50.260
And I get angry because I feel
it's dismissed all the time.

10:50.260 --> 10:52.810
And I don't mean to
personalize it just to me

10:52.810 --> 10:54.886
because I see it happen to my brothers

10:54.886 --> 10:59.520
and sisters within the
community as well, so.

10:59.520 --> 11:02.380
But I think we get it from all sides.

11:02.380 --> 11:06.083
And you know, in
employment, it's very clear,

11:08.180 --> 11:11.500
we are not white men, so it's very clear,

11:11.500 --> 11:14.833
that hierarchy, and I
think pay is affected.

11:15.700 --> 11:18.130
I work for a historically black college.

11:18.130 --> 11:19.990
So I think that's a
little bit differently,

11:19.990 --> 11:22.283
but I think race plays a part there.

11:25.070 --> 11:28.980
In any job environment where you interact

11:28.980 --> 11:31.203
with people of other races and ethnicity.

11:32.900 --> 11:36.610
I feel so marginalized a lot of times

11:36.610 --> 11:40.840
by people and things and
my life experience that,

11:40.840 --> 11:44.500
yeah, it felt like a milestone
to be asked to go to Harvard

11:44.500 --> 11:47.980
because people usually
diss my intelligence

11:47.980 --> 11:49.700
on a daily basis.

11:49.700 --> 11:54.700
They do do due to my race or
because I'm a transsexual.

11:54.770 --> 11:57.273
And so I'm discounted as a person.

11:58.520 --> 12:01.273
It's hard for me when I practice, here,

12:03.060 --> 12:08.060
in the city I live in, because
of the negativity of that.

12:08.310 --> 12:11.130
And one of my clients go
to court with me one day

12:11.130 --> 12:11.963
and he comes out, he goes,

12:11.963 --> 12:15.270
"I don't know how you stand
that on a daily basis."

12:15.270 --> 12:17.910
And just the, there is the hate there.

12:17.910 --> 12:20.170
there is the how dare you?

12:20.170 --> 12:22.620
I did a case the other day,
and I know the other attorney

12:22.620 --> 12:27.150
left angry and crying because
a transsexual black person

12:27.150 --> 12:30.690
beat her, you know, and
it didn't dawn on me,

12:30.690 --> 12:32.960
'cause that's not the
first thing I wanna think.

12:32.960 --> 12:34.890
But that's, you know,
why, 'cause she had gone

12:34.890 --> 12:37.830
to law school with me,
she had graduated with me,

12:37.830 --> 12:41.240
the whole elitist, you know,
even in law school things,

12:41.240 --> 12:45.430
she was part of that
whole elitist echelon.

12:45.430 --> 12:50.430
So how is this black person
and this transsexual person

12:51.270 --> 12:54.910
gonna even be able to be
as intelligent as I am?

12:54.910 --> 12:58.270
So that's where it comes from to be like,

12:58.270 --> 13:01.130
well, you know, I get invited to Harvard

13:01.130 --> 13:03.083
and I speak at Harvard Law School,

13:04.218 --> 13:08.130
when most people won't
even hire me for a job

13:08.130 --> 13:10.053
or treat me horribly when they do.

13:11.724 --> 13:13.740
I have a whole theory around passing,

13:13.740 --> 13:16.540
having been high yellow, and
that's what it was called

13:16.540 --> 13:19.660
back in my day is that
I don't like passing,

13:19.660 --> 13:21.550
which is why I do come out to people.

13:21.550 --> 13:24.170
I'm very proud to be a black American.

13:24.170 --> 13:26.190
I'm very proud to be who I am.

13:26.190 --> 13:28.540
And I think that if you're
not proud to be who you are,

13:28.540 --> 13:31.330
people can use that as power against you.

13:31.330 --> 13:33.470
And plus why wouldn't you love yourself

13:33.470 --> 13:36.170
or be who you are and embrace who you are?

13:36.170 --> 13:39.130
So I think that's very
important for people to do.

13:39.130 --> 13:40.990
And I never looked back a day.

13:40.990 --> 13:42.670
People ask me, do you regret?

13:42.670 --> 13:44.480
'Cause I had friends,
they worried, you know,

13:44.480 --> 13:46.340
they're two gay guys,
every time they're like,

13:46.340 --> 13:47.440
are you regret, do you regret?

13:47.440 --> 13:52.280
I've never regretted one day my decision,

13:52.280 --> 13:54.397
because it was life or
death for me at that point,

13:54.397 --> 13:58.470
and it wouldn't be worth living
if I wasn't living who I am.

13:58.470 --> 14:00.780
And there have been tons of hard times

14:00.780 --> 14:05.375
and not being paid enough
and barely making ends meet

14:05.375 --> 14:09.830
and feeling limited in job
choices because of who I am,

14:09.830 --> 14:13.700
but I still wouldn't trade being myself.

14:13.700 --> 14:15.093
So at all.

14:17.845 --> 14:20.512
(upbeat music)

14:23.280 --> 14:28.170
- My family is pretty,
well, they think that,

14:28.170 --> 14:29.440
they say that they're liberal,

14:29.440 --> 14:30.690
but they're pretty conservative

14:30.690 --> 14:31.730
when it comes right down to it.

14:31.730 --> 14:34.420
They had a really hard time,
'cause before I transitioned,

14:34.420 --> 14:37.310
I came out as a lesbian
when I was really young

14:38.880 --> 14:41.500
and they had a really hard time with that.

14:41.500 --> 14:44.920
And then when I decided
that I needed to transition,

14:44.920 --> 14:48.420
they, I don't, my father,

14:48.420 --> 14:51.763
I haven't really talked
about transition with.

14:51.763 --> 14:55.940
I think he knows, but we
just don't talk about it.

14:55.940 --> 14:58.250
I remember asking my father
when I was about three years old

14:58.250 --> 15:00.110
when my penis was gonna grow

15:00.110 --> 15:04.280
and it totally freaked him
out, totally freaked him out.

15:04.280 --> 15:08.460
But I knew, like I don't
know, I would see little boys

15:10.410 --> 15:13.660
and just always wanted to be with them.

15:13.660 --> 15:14.900
You know, always wanted to do the things

15:14.900 --> 15:17.150
that they were doing,
always wanted to hang out

15:17.150 --> 15:19.260
with them, you know, all the time.

15:19.260 --> 15:21.840
And I hated having to
wear the things that,

15:21.840 --> 15:23.650
you know, the dresses and whatnot.

15:23.650 --> 15:25.926
And especially when you're
two and three years old,

15:25.926 --> 15:28.080
you kinda have to wear
whatever your parents

15:28.080 --> 15:28.930
put on you, you know?

15:28.930 --> 15:30.630
And I would always be ripping stuff off

15:30.630 --> 15:33.420
and wanting to go around
in just a diaper, you know,

15:33.420 --> 15:36.520
and just couldn't understand
why I couldn't just wear,

15:36.520 --> 15:38.800
you know, a pair of shorts
and a t-shirt, you know,

15:38.800 --> 15:40.440
like everybody else was.

15:40.440 --> 15:43.880
Well, one time we had to
go to a choir rehearsal

15:43.880 --> 15:48.310
and he had this blue,
this powder blue pantsuit

15:48.310 --> 15:50.000
that he bought for me,
he wanted me to wear it.

15:50.000 --> 15:51.950
So I did, you know, and we went,

15:51.950 --> 15:53.600
we got to the rehearsal
and there's this woman,

15:53.600 --> 15:55.210
this like 60 year old woman wearing

15:55.210 --> 15:57.530
the exact same thing that I was wearing.

15:57.530 --> 15:59.610
And it just freaked me out.

15:59.610 --> 16:00.802
And I was like, I got in the car.

16:00.802 --> 16:02.640
I'm like, there's no way I'm
ever wearing this thing again.

16:02.640 --> 16:04.030
I'm never gonna wear this again.

16:04.030 --> 16:07.360
And so I got home and,
you know, we did laundry

16:07.360 --> 16:08.737
and washed it and everything, and I said,

16:08.737 --> 16:10.597
"Well, I need to iron this, you know,

16:10.597 --> 16:12.387
"just to make sure that it looks nice

16:12.387 --> 16:13.690
"the next time I wear it."

16:13.690 --> 16:16.063
So I accidentally on
purpose, burned a hole in it

16:16.063 --> 16:18.440
with the iron so that I
wouldn't have to wear it again.

16:18.440 --> 16:20.950
And I mean, you know,
he acted like it was,

16:20.950 --> 16:22.460
you know, I acted like
it was a big accident.

16:22.460 --> 16:26.720
And he was like, he knew
that I didn't wanna wear it,

16:26.720 --> 16:29.683
but he never bought anything
like that for me again.

16:30.580 --> 16:34.780
But yeah, childhood
was interesting for me.

16:34.780 --> 16:36.310
It was very interesting for me.

16:36.310 --> 16:38.260
I have, I was born with cerebral palsy.

16:39.530 --> 16:43.310
It mostly affects my legs,
but I do have some involvement

16:43.310 --> 16:48.310
in my hands and it makes
it difficult to walk.

16:48.530 --> 16:52.020
Before my transition,
I had like 27 surgeries

16:52.020 --> 16:53.810
to try to straighten out my legs

16:53.810 --> 16:56.610
and straighten out my back and all that.

16:56.610 --> 17:00.780
So I spent a good deal of
my childhood in a hospital.

17:00.780 --> 17:04.570
So I didn't really, I wasn't really social

17:04.570 --> 17:06.483
in ways that other kids are.

17:08.340 --> 17:11.850
And so I didn't, I don't,
I never really learned

17:11.850 --> 17:16.130
how to do those, you know,
like be, make friends,

17:16.130 --> 17:18.090
you know, in school and stuff like that.

17:18.090 --> 17:23.090
So now I'm sort of, you know,
sort of learning those things.

17:23.620 --> 17:24.930
There's a lot of things that I don't know

17:24.930 --> 17:28.430
about how to interact with people as male,

17:28.430 --> 17:30.790
because I was socialized as female.

17:30.790 --> 17:35.300
So I kind of, I tend to defer more.

17:35.300 --> 17:38.170
Like, I don't know.

17:38.170 --> 17:43.170
When someone's talking to me,
if I'm talking with a woman,

17:43.510 --> 17:45.780
I tend not to look at them as much

17:45.780 --> 17:48.190
as I do when I'm talking to a man.

17:48.190 --> 17:50.730
And I don't know what, I'm
not sure whether that's about,

17:50.730 --> 17:52.320
although I don't look at men.

17:52.320 --> 17:53.777
I mean, I do look at men like, you know,

17:53.777 --> 17:55.227
I'm a gay man, I look at men,

17:56.570 --> 17:59.600
but I don't really look them in the eye

17:59.600 --> 18:02.370
as much as I would like to.

18:02.370 --> 18:05.310
And so I feel like I'm not
being taken seriously a lot

18:05.310 --> 18:06.830
because I can't, I have a hard time

18:06.830 --> 18:09.630
looking people in the
eye because as a woman,

18:09.630 --> 18:12.840
I was always taught
that you lower your eyes

18:12.840 --> 18:15.320
when people are speaking to
you, lower your eyes a bit.

18:15.320 --> 18:18.570
And so I'm still trying to
get used to just looking

18:18.570 --> 18:21.160
at people full in the face, you know?

18:21.160 --> 18:22.693
And that's been hard.

18:24.890 --> 18:28.390
In terms of, but I think
also being a black man

18:28.390 --> 18:31.940
is very different than
being a black woman.

18:31.940 --> 18:35.470
And so I'm learning
sort of how to interact

18:35.470 --> 18:37.300
with people that way.

18:37.300 --> 18:41.573
A lot of people are afraid to approach me

18:41.573 --> 18:43.720
because they see black
man, and they're like,

18:43.720 --> 18:46.730
okay, what's he gonna
do to me kinda thing.

18:46.730 --> 18:49.024
You know, and what am I, you know,

18:49.024 --> 18:50.850
when I think about me, it's like,

18:50.850 --> 18:53.080
what am I going to do
to anybody, you know?

18:53.080 --> 18:56.980
I mean, I can remember last
winter getting stuck somewhere

18:56.980 --> 18:59.340
in my power chair and wanting, you know,

18:59.340 --> 19:01.020
looking for someone to stop and help me.

19:01.020 --> 19:01.853
And nobody would stop.

19:01.853 --> 19:02.720
It's like, what am I going to do?

19:02.720 --> 19:05.160
Carjack you from my wheelchair, you know?

19:05.160 --> 19:09.263
I mean, and all I needed
was a push out of the snow.

19:10.200 --> 19:11.832
Finally someone stopped, and,

19:11.832 --> 19:13.300
but they wouldn't get out of the car.

19:13.300 --> 19:14.430
They rolled down the window and said,

19:14.430 --> 19:15.690
do you want me to call the police?

19:15.690 --> 19:18.063
It's like, no, if you
would just come push me,

19:18.063 --> 19:20.380
I could get where I need to go, you know?

19:20.380 --> 19:22.573
'Cause I was like a block from my house,

19:23.740 --> 19:24.930
it was like, you call the police,

19:24.930 --> 19:27.330
it's gonna take them 45
minutes to get out here,

19:27.330 --> 19:29.800
and they're going to do
exactly the same thing,

19:29.800 --> 19:32.360
but I'm gonna be frozen, you know?

19:32.360 --> 19:35.520
So I don't get that.

19:35.520 --> 19:38.510
I don't understand like how that works,

19:38.510 --> 19:42.770
and how someone gets used to
being perceived in that way.

19:42.770 --> 19:44.790
You know, I just don't understand it,

19:44.790 --> 19:48.143
but I'm learning how to deal with it.

19:49.070 --> 19:51.800
It's hard being a black
man in this society.

19:51.800 --> 19:55.013
And I never really recognized that before,

19:55.920 --> 19:59.010
but now that I am one, I see the,

19:59.010 --> 20:02.720
the oppression and the
prejudice that people

20:02.720 --> 20:03.887
have towards black men.

20:03.887 --> 20:08.594
And it's very scary, and I
also recognize that in myself,

20:08.594 --> 20:10.640
that I have some of that in myself

20:10.640 --> 20:14.005
and I'm trying to change that.

20:14.005 --> 20:17.490
I have a lot of internalized
racism and prejudice,

20:17.490 --> 20:19.810
and I'm trying to change that.

20:19.810 --> 20:23.043
In terms of the way that the
media portrays black men,

20:25.210 --> 20:27.850
I have a lotta issues, a
lotta issues around that

20:27.850 --> 20:32.256
because I know that
I'm perceived that way,

20:32.256 --> 20:36.720
in a lot of situations, and I'm not that.

20:36.720 --> 20:39.500
I mean, most of the time, it's either,

20:39.500 --> 20:41.870
you either see black men being portrayed

20:41.870 --> 20:46.650
as superstar athlete or criminal,

20:46.650 --> 20:48.930
and there's nothing in between.

20:48.930 --> 20:50.440
And I have a really hard time with that

20:50.440 --> 20:54.670
because I'm not, obviously
not a superstar athlete,

20:54.670 --> 20:56.850
but I'm also not a criminal, you know?

20:56.850 --> 21:00.340
And the only other representation

21:00.340 --> 21:02.810
that I've seen lately is Barack Obama,

21:02.810 --> 21:05.100
and I'm not that, either.

21:05.100 --> 21:08.540
So I think that it's really important

21:10.080 --> 21:12.870
to get a different
perspective on what black men,

21:12.870 --> 21:15.220
who black men are, you know?

21:15.220 --> 21:18.150
It's been a long struggle
for me to figure out

21:18.150 --> 21:20.270
what kinda man I wanna be.

21:20.270 --> 21:24.750
And basically what I
discovered is that I'm already

21:24.750 --> 21:27.330
the man that I wanna be,
I just have to live it.

21:27.330 --> 21:29.850
I'm not doing this to
make my life easy or hard.

21:29.850 --> 21:33.220
It's not, you know, it's
not a choice that I have.

21:33.220 --> 21:35.470
It's something that I have to do,

21:35.470 --> 21:38.200
in order to be comfortable and to live.

21:38.200 --> 21:41.810
Who's to say, who can really say

21:41.810 --> 21:43.930
whether this is real for you?

21:43.930 --> 21:45.640
Who can say whether it's real for you?

21:45.640 --> 21:47.550
Only you can say that.

21:47.550 --> 21:49.910
No doctor's really gonna
be able to tell me that,

21:49.910 --> 21:53.410
no, you don't have, no,
you're not a transsexual.

21:53.410 --> 21:55.830
Well, yeah, I am, you know?

21:55.830 --> 21:58.670
You can't tell me that I'm not, you know,

21:58.670 --> 22:00.960
unless you live in my body, you can't,

22:00.960 --> 22:03.103
nobody can know but me, so.

22:04.200 --> 22:07.970
And it took a good three
years before I got my surgery,

22:07.970 --> 22:09.480
my chest surgery done.

22:09.480 --> 22:13.740
But it was, that was
the hardest three years

22:13.740 --> 22:15.316
that I've ever spent.

22:15.316 --> 22:18.000
The hysterectomy wasn't
as important to me,

22:18.000 --> 22:20.580
although I did wanna have it
done, it wasn't as important.

22:20.580 --> 22:23.106
'Cause you can't see that, you know?

22:23.106 --> 22:27.290
But having breasts was just like,

22:27.290 --> 22:29.150
this is not what a guy's
supposed to look like, you know?

22:29.150 --> 22:31.280
'Cause I mean, I had a,
starting to get a beard,

22:31.280 --> 22:33.830
and I had a mustache and
stuff and was looking

22:33.830 --> 22:36.530
a lot more masculine and then,
there's these big honking,

22:36.530 --> 22:40.670
you know, and it's just like,
ugh, no, no, can't do it.

22:40.670 --> 22:41.570
(laughs) You know?

22:43.650 --> 22:48.353
I think sexuality and gender
are very much separate,

22:49.530 --> 22:54.510
and I've always been attracted
to men, but I wasn't really,

22:54.510 --> 22:56.210
I wasn't attracted to straight men.

22:56.210 --> 22:59.320
Like I could never imagine
myself being in that kind

22:59.320 --> 23:02.270
of a relationship where I was
perceived as being straight,

23:02.270 --> 23:03.553
just, I couldn't do it.

23:05.870 --> 23:10.870
But I knew that the men
that I was attracted to,

23:11.210 --> 23:13.050
'cause I was mostly attracted to gay men,

23:13.050 --> 23:15.720
they wouldn't wanna be
with me in the body,

23:15.720 --> 23:17.650
the way that my body was.

23:17.650 --> 23:19.140
Now that I've transitioned, and so,

23:19.140 --> 23:22.330
I figured ,I wanted to be a
part of the gay community.

23:22.330 --> 23:25.763
So I lived as a lesbian for a long time,

23:25.763 --> 23:29.590
just so mainly so that I can
be part of the community,

23:29.590 --> 23:31.700
but I did fall in love with a woman.

23:31.700 --> 23:35.630
And that was really difficult for me when,

23:35.630 --> 23:38.210
'cause she died of cervical cancer

23:38.210 --> 23:42.520
and it was really difficult
for me to reconcile the fact

23:42.520 --> 23:44.910
that I know that I've
always been a gay man

23:44.910 --> 23:47.410
with the fact that I fell
in love with this woman,

23:49.721 --> 23:54.721
but it ended up working out
okay, in terms of, we both,

23:55.070 --> 23:58.240
we loved each other and that's
all that matters, really.

23:58.240 --> 24:03.140
But really, love is the only
thing that really matters

24:03.140 --> 24:06.210
and you can't really help
who you fall in love with.

24:06.210 --> 24:10.190
So me falling in love with this woman was,

24:10.190 --> 24:12.810
it was disconcerting, but at
the same time, it was love.

24:12.810 --> 24:15.710
So, you know, what are
you, how can you beat that?

24:15.710 --> 24:18.650
But I don't think that,
I think that my sexuality

24:18.650 --> 24:21.370
really hadn't changed, hasn't changed

24:21.370 --> 24:23.770
because I've always been gay.

24:23.770 --> 24:26.490
You know, I just, I was
with a woman for awhile

24:26.490 --> 24:29.390
and now I'm I'm with men, you know,

24:29.390 --> 24:31.850
and most of the men that I've been with

24:31.850 --> 24:34.280
since I transitioned
were, have been trans men,

24:34.280 --> 24:37.710
but I'm not gonna rule out the possibility

24:37.710 --> 24:41.170
of being with a bio male, either.

24:41.170 --> 24:45.840
'Cause that, that's like,
would be fun, you know?

24:45.840 --> 24:48.450
'Cause it's always fun, you know?

24:48.450 --> 24:52.780
And for me, as long as I'm
not hurting anybody else,

24:52.780 --> 24:54.780
you know, unless they asked me to,

24:54.780 --> 24:59.023
and I'm not doing anything that's illegal,

25:00.118 --> 25:03.543
if it feels good, I'm gonna do it, so.

25:07.150 --> 25:09.940
I've always wanted, I
always wanted tattoos

25:09.940 --> 25:12.250
from the time that I was
really, really young.

25:12.250 --> 25:16.110
And I just decided that I
was gonna do it one day.

25:16.110 --> 25:19.990
So on my 18th birthday,
actually I went and got a tattoo

25:19.990 --> 25:23.130
for myself and my parents
kinda freaked out about it,

25:23.130 --> 25:25.223
you know, the whole, that whole thing.

25:26.127 --> 25:27.960
But I figured, you know, this is like,

25:27.960 --> 25:30.578
it's a coming of age
kind of thing, you know?

25:30.578 --> 25:34.630
And guys do that when they
turn 18, they get tattoos.

25:34.630 --> 25:36.250
So I'm gonna get a tattoo.

25:36.250 --> 25:39.410
You know, I'm a guy, I'm
18, I'm gonna get a tattoo.

25:39.410 --> 25:40.470
So that's what I did.

25:40.470 --> 25:43.330
The piercings, that I was, (chuckling)

25:43.330 --> 25:46.490
that I've been doing a lot longer.

25:46.490 --> 25:48.230
I mean, I wanted to get my ears pierced

25:48.230 --> 25:50.100
when I was real little, but my
father wouldn't let me do it

25:50.100 --> 25:54.160
until I was like 15, and
then, once I turned 18,

25:54.160 --> 25:57.360
it was just like, I went wild and I mean,

25:57.360 --> 26:00.870
I had like 20 piercings in one ear

26:00.870 --> 26:05.350
and then I got this huge plug put in,

26:05.350 --> 26:07.950
and my eyebrows, I had
both my eyebrows pierced.

26:07.950 --> 26:11.370
I had my lip pierced all along here.

26:11.370 --> 26:13.730
I had my nose pierced in my septum,

26:13.730 --> 26:15.623
and both nostrils pierced.

26:17.252 --> 26:21.570
I had surface piercings
along my neck for a while.

26:21.570 --> 26:24.290
It was just, and I mean, that again

26:24.290 --> 26:26.770
is another spiritual thing,
tattoos and piercings.

26:26.770 --> 26:28.260
They take me to another place

26:28.260 --> 26:30.840
and it's just like a
connection with something

26:30.840 --> 26:33.700
that's so outside of myself, you know,

26:33.700 --> 26:38.520
I feel just euphoric when I get them done.

26:38.520 --> 26:41.000
I absolutely love them and I will do them

26:41.000 --> 26:42.430
until the day I die.

26:42.430 --> 26:46.826
I know that I will always get,
whenever I have enough money,

26:46.826 --> 26:49.710
you know, after all my
stuff, all my necessities

26:49.710 --> 26:52.630
and all the obligations
that I have are done,

26:52.630 --> 26:55.800
if I have any extra money,
that's where it goes.

26:55.800 --> 26:57.510
I don't even buy books that much anymore,

26:57.510 --> 26:59.517
and I love to read, but I don't
buy books that much anymore

26:59.517 --> 27:02.210
because I wanna get pierced and tattooed.

27:02.210 --> 27:05.347
You know, it's just, it's
that important to me, so.

27:07.826 --> 27:10.493
(upbeat music)

27:14.742 --> 27:16.518
- We ask that you'd bless
this service today, Lord.

27:16.518 --> 27:17.351
- [Congregation] Yes.

27:17.351 --> 27:19.611
- That you'd bless each and
every one who's here today,

27:19.611 --> 27:21.658
Lord, there are some
here who have the hurt,

27:21.658 --> 27:23.048
some here who have pain.

27:23.048 --> 27:25.440
Lord, we ask that you'd mend
their hearts today, Lord.

27:25.440 --> 27:27.690
Mend their bodies today, Lord.

27:27.690 --> 27:29.312
Mend their spirits today, Lord,

27:29.312 --> 27:30.784
in the name of Jesus.

27:30.784 --> 27:32.852
We ask that the Word goes forth, Lord,

27:32.852 --> 27:34.410
today, in the name of Jesus.

27:34.410 --> 27:36.357
We ask that you have your glory shine

27:36.357 --> 27:38.809
through your words today, Lord Jesus.

27:38.809 --> 27:40.685
We ask that you bless our pastor today,

27:40.685 --> 27:42.809
as she delivers the Word today, Lord.

27:42.809 --> 27:45.178
We ask that you'd bless the
musicians here today, Lord.

27:45.178 --> 27:47.050
We ask that you'd bless
our praise team, Lord,

27:47.050 --> 27:49.684
as they sing praises to your name.

27:49.684 --> 27:50.517
As always, Lord, we give you the praise,

27:51.827 --> 27:53.590
to you, the honor and the glory.

27:53.590 --> 27:55.173
In your Son, Jesus' name, we pray.

27:55.173 --> 27:56.206
- Worthy!
- Amen.

27:56.206 --> 27:57.558
- [Congregation] Amen.

27:57.558 --> 28:01.990
(congregation cheering)
(congregation applauding)

28:01.990 --> 28:03.960
- I've had this calling for a long time.

28:03.960 --> 28:07.830
I'm just one of those who,
who's fighting the call,

28:07.830 --> 28:12.110
'cause like I say, I'm a
behind the scenes person

28:12.110 --> 28:14.610
and now, God is trying to push me forward.

28:14.610 --> 28:17.260
And I'm like, I don't, you know, but I,

28:17.260 --> 28:21.650
I do what I'm supposed to do,
but I'm doing it begrudgingly.

28:21.650 --> 28:22.483
We live our name.

28:22.483 --> 28:23.680
When we say we were a pillar of love

28:23.680 --> 28:26.510
'cause people always say
that they, they feel a love

28:26.510 --> 28:28.610
when we come through, when
they come through the doors

28:28.610 --> 28:31.090
and it's genuine.

28:31.090 --> 28:34.560
There's people who've called
the church, a pillar of hope

28:34.560 --> 28:38.080
a pillar of life, and I
tell them, you know what?

28:38.080 --> 28:42.600
Don't even worry about
that because folks there,

28:42.600 --> 28:44.100
that's what you're feeling.

28:44.100 --> 28:46.690
You're feeling that
you've come to this church

28:46.690 --> 28:49.372
and folks are coming half dead.

28:49.372 --> 28:51.360
But you come here and
you're feeling a love

28:51.360 --> 28:53.960
and you're feeling alive again.

28:53.960 --> 28:56.130
There's people who come,
they're looking for hope

28:56.130 --> 28:58.770
and they find that hope and
they, and they find that love

28:58.770 --> 29:02.587
to say, "Wow, I can be
gay, bisexual, transgender,

29:02.587 --> 29:04.550
"and still have a relationship with God."

29:04.550 --> 29:07.710
Whatever you identify as,
whatever your religion is,

29:07.710 --> 29:10.720
or you don't have a religion
or your spirituality,

29:10.720 --> 29:15.223
we welcome all, and it's a good place.

29:17.360 --> 29:19.493
I'm glad that I'm there.

29:20.850 --> 29:23.810
My kids have grown up in the church,

29:23.810 --> 29:25.330
which I've always wanted.

29:25.330 --> 29:29.920
I've never grew up in the
church, so this is new for me.

29:29.920 --> 29:31.840
My mother always sent me to church,

29:31.840 --> 29:32.870
but she never went to church.

29:32.870 --> 29:33.810
I'm like, that's not right.

29:33.810 --> 29:36.017
You know, how you gonna tell
me I need to go to church

29:36.017 --> 29:38.160
and you're not going to church yourself?

29:38.160 --> 29:40.930
But for all of us, my wife and my kids,

29:40.930 --> 29:44.270
to all go as a family, it's,
it's a wonderful thing.

29:44.270 --> 29:49.270
We met at church and she
tells me when she saw me,

29:49.840 --> 29:54.193
she thought I was a guy,
but she thought I was a,

29:55.330 --> 29:57.590
what did you say?
- Flaming.

29:57.590 --> 30:00.230
- A flaming guy? (laughs)

30:00.230 --> 30:01.630
Like why I gotta be flaming?

30:02.600 --> 30:03.900
She thought I was a flaming guy.

30:03.900 --> 30:07.450
And when I was preaching the one Sunday

30:07.450 --> 30:09.400
and my mom and uncle were there,

30:09.400 --> 30:11.687
so, you know, I said,
you know, I told them,

30:11.687 --> 30:15.772
"My name is Deacon Carl, and
for those of you who are here,

30:15.772 --> 30:19.570
"my family is here, knows me as Caroline."

30:19.570 --> 30:22.040
And that's when she was
like, "Oh, you're a female."

30:22.040 --> 30:25.710
And she was all smiles.

30:25.710 --> 30:28.150
And afterwards, you know,
you greet everybody.

30:28.150 --> 30:28.983
She's all smiles and was like,

30:28.983 --> 30:31.187
"Ooh, I really enjoyed your sermon.

30:31.187 --> 30:34.360
"And do you have any discussions
and stuff like that?"

30:34.360 --> 30:36.950
And I'm like, well, yeah,
you know, we, you know,

30:36.950 --> 30:39.180
we can talk or whatever like that.

30:39.180 --> 30:40.420
A lot of back and forth with us.

30:40.420 --> 30:45.420
We ended up finally getting
together after a year or two.

30:46.240 --> 30:48.350
And my mother was ill.

30:48.350 --> 30:49.880
She was diabetic and she was ill,

30:49.880 --> 30:52.100
and she couldn't get around a lot.

30:52.100 --> 30:54.110
And what meant a lot to me,
and I think it meant a lot

30:54.110 --> 30:58.183
to my mother was the Christmas
before my mother passed,

30:59.120 --> 31:01.630
Wanda took my mother
out Christmas shopping

31:01.630 --> 31:03.180
and no one would've ever had the patience

31:03.180 --> 31:04.930
to take my mother Christmas shopping.

31:04.930 --> 31:07.670
And she, you know, she would
always just give me money.

31:07.670 --> 31:08.710
And I knew it meant to her

31:08.710 --> 31:10.860
that she would wanna
go Christmas shopping.

31:10.860 --> 31:13.610
And Wanda took her and
nobody else would think,

31:13.610 --> 31:15.550
well, when we get there,
put her in the wheelchair.

31:15.550 --> 31:16.664
You know?

31:16.664 --> 31:17.497
And that's what they did.

31:17.497 --> 31:18.330
They went to Target.

31:18.330 --> 31:20.430
They, she pushed in the wheelchair
and they had a good time.

31:20.430 --> 31:21.700
And then it meant a lot to my mother,

31:21.700 --> 31:23.450
'cause she was able to do that.

31:23.450 --> 31:27.200
And we went to the show in January.

31:27.200 --> 31:29.680
And for her birthday, she
loved Denzel Washington.

31:29.680 --> 31:32.051
So we went to see Denzel Washington.

31:32.051 --> 31:33.940
And unfortunately, then,
the following months,

31:33.940 --> 31:35.990
she just, she passed away suddenly.

31:35.990 --> 31:38.390
But it just, it really meant a lot to me

31:40.560 --> 31:42.280
what Wanda did for my mother,

31:42.280 --> 31:45.440
'cause no other woman has
ever done that for her.

31:45.440 --> 31:47.710
And even when my mother passed,

31:47.710 --> 31:51.320
and Wanda and I had a
falling out that weekend,

31:51.320 --> 31:54.440
and my mother passed and I
told her I was at the hospital.

31:54.440 --> 31:55.580
I said, "My mom died."

31:55.580 --> 31:56.413
And she was like, "Where are you?

31:56.413 --> 31:57.680
"Where are you," I just hung up the phone,

31:57.680 --> 31:59.450
'cause I didn't want to talk about it.

31:59.450 --> 32:03.062
And next thing I know, she
done popped up at the (laughs),

32:03.062 --> 32:04.683
I was like, "How you know where I was?"

32:04.683 --> 32:09.657
And God spoke to me and says,
"This is gonna be your wife,

32:09.657 --> 32:10.920
"whether you like it or not,"

32:10.920 --> 32:12.490
just like I am with my ministry,

32:12.490 --> 32:15.400
this is going to be your wife, and we got

32:15.400 --> 32:18.897
our domestic partnership a
year later and we decided

32:20.873 --> 32:25.163
to have kids 'cause I'm like,
I'm getting old. I need to.

32:27.111 --> 32:31.075
And they gave us a 0.07% chance

32:31.075 --> 32:35.440
that we would have our
babies and, or a baby.

32:35.440 --> 32:37.930
'Cause before, we always
talked about one baby

32:37.930 --> 32:42.343
and lo and behold two
came, and the next thing,

32:42.343 --> 32:44.220
I found out they were
girls, I was like, see,

32:44.220 --> 32:46.300
I got to get another job,
'cause I got three women

32:46.300 --> 32:48.042
in the house now.
- The pregnancy-

32:48.042 --> 32:49.090
- Nah, it's not gonna work.

32:49.090 --> 32:51.200
- I had successfully become pregnant.

32:51.200 --> 32:53.820
But my, my journey-
- Tell them how.

32:53.820 --> 32:55.530
Right, they took my eggs.

32:55.530 --> 32:59.910
- Oh yeah, they took
his eggs fertilized it

32:59.910 --> 33:03.093
with anonymous sperm that
we got from a sperm bank,

33:03.093 --> 33:06.420
and then implanted them in me.

33:06.420 --> 33:10.460
So this is when they told
us we got 0.7% chance.

33:10.460 --> 33:11.293
Okay.

33:11.293 --> 33:14.330
So we get up, we get past the first month.

33:14.330 --> 33:17.380
we get past the second
month and they're like,

33:17.380 --> 33:19.700
wow, you shouldn't be this, you know,

33:19.700 --> 33:21.300
it should be terminated right now.

33:21.300 --> 33:23.530
And I'm feeling all these weird pains.

33:23.530 --> 33:25.250
And then we get to the third month,

33:25.250 --> 33:27.240
and then we get to the fourth month,

33:27.240 --> 33:30.320
I'm like, you know,
people, I'm really sure

33:30.320 --> 33:32.700
that I should not be feeling these pains.

33:32.700 --> 33:34.900
You know, everybody thought I was crazy.

33:34.900 --> 33:36.940
And I'm like, no, I'm pretty sure

33:36.940 --> 33:39.020
I'm not supposed to be feeling this.

33:39.020 --> 33:43.440
Come to find out I was
in labor at four months.

33:43.440 --> 33:44.273
Yeah.

33:47.021 --> 33:51.700
So I had to be put on
bedrest from my fourth month,

33:51.700 --> 33:54.930
all the way to the time I had the babies

33:54.930 --> 33:57.830
and they had to sew me up,

33:57.830 --> 34:00.870
giving me this thing called us a cerclage.

34:00.870 --> 34:04.760
And that was very trying
on our relationship

34:04.760 --> 34:07.080
because I couldn't do anything.

34:07.080 --> 34:08.770
I had to get permission just to go

34:08.770 --> 34:10.460
to the bathroom for myself.

34:10.460 --> 34:15.460
So, you know, I needed around
the clock, you know, help.

34:16.370 --> 34:20.080
And you know, our church
family came together

34:20.080 --> 34:24.000
like you wouldn't believe,
and they all supported us

34:24.000 --> 34:26.696
and they would come
give me meals, you know,

34:26.696 --> 34:30.070
try to do like our dishes
and things like that

34:30.070 --> 34:34.680
because he's used to me doing
everything and you know,

34:34.680 --> 34:37.170
otherwise our house look
a mess all the time.

34:37.170 --> 34:38.720
So they had to do that.

34:38.720 --> 34:41.690
So both came out perfectly fine

34:42.540 --> 34:45.110
through prayer and hard work.

34:45.110 --> 34:46.740
- Definitely prayer.

34:46.740 --> 34:48.500
- Yeah.
- Definitely.

34:48.500 --> 34:49.520
- Yeah.

34:49.520 --> 34:54.170
So that, I mean that story,
it's a little bit more detailed,

34:54.170 --> 34:57.640
but as you can see, it's priceless, yeah.

34:57.640 --> 35:02.010
Yeah, and we want to
help other other couples.

35:02.010 --> 35:04.690
So we were thinking about
putting a book out there

35:04.690 --> 35:08.300
to help couples, help conceive,

35:08.300 --> 35:12.170
and everything because why
shouldn't we be able to have

35:12.170 --> 35:14.510
the perfect family, like everybody else,

35:14.510 --> 35:17.400
like the heterosexuals, you know?

35:17.400 --> 35:19.490
We don't have a white picket fence yet,

35:19.490 --> 35:22.480
but pretty much we got everything else.

35:25.120 --> 35:27.930
- Like I said, she's, through all my BS,

35:27.930 --> 35:32.930
Wanda has, has been there
for me, and I don't know.

35:33.035 --> 35:36.310
I think she crazy, but
I thank God for her,

35:36.310 --> 35:38.730
that through all of our mess, you know,

35:38.730 --> 35:41.850
she's been with me and
we're still together.

35:41.850 --> 35:43.780
You know, we have our ups and our downs,

35:43.780 --> 35:46.050
but we're trying to work things out,

35:46.050 --> 35:50.000
and get together and do this thing

35:50.000 --> 35:52.910
'cause we don't wanna be separated,

35:52.910 --> 35:55.710
and we don't want our kids
to grow up in separate homes.

35:56.680 --> 35:58.830
We want them to have
a mother and a father.

36:00.808 --> 36:04.350
I have a little chauvinist in me

36:04.350 --> 36:09.350
and I'm trying to get over
that and, and learning that

36:11.790 --> 36:13.810
your woman's not supposed
to do everything,

36:13.810 --> 36:17.760
but it's hard, and it's
funny because people say,

36:17.760 --> 36:19.530
well, where did you get this idea?

36:19.530 --> 36:21.800
Because your uncles are not like that.

36:21.800 --> 36:23.440
And it's watching TV.

36:23.440 --> 36:24.830
You know, my mother even told me,

36:24.830 --> 36:27.690
you need a 1950s woman because you know,

36:27.690 --> 36:29.080
you want everybody to
do this and it's true.

36:29.080 --> 36:31.300
I wanted, you know, you
stay home with the kids.

36:31.300 --> 36:33.720
I come home, bring home
the bacon, woo-wop, da bam.

36:33.720 --> 36:35.460
But that's not working.

36:35.460 --> 36:38.030
It's like no, I'm not getting
paid enough money to do this.

36:38.030 --> 36:42.550
So I have to learn how to,
and it's a learning process,

36:42.550 --> 36:45.330
learn how to make my wife my equal.

36:45.330 --> 36:47.420
You know, she's not in
front of me, behind me.

36:47.420 --> 36:48.710
She's next to me.

36:48.710 --> 36:51.860
And it's weird that I feeling myself

36:51.860 --> 36:55.203
being pulled towards being
an activist for women.

36:56.130 --> 36:59.480
Actually my pastor's partner, she works

37:00.750 --> 37:04.070
for a rape crisis hotline,
and I've been feeling myself

37:04.070 --> 37:07.810
being pulled to, to be there,
work with her with that.

37:07.810 --> 37:10.570
And I had actually
asked her about training

37:10.570 --> 37:12.100
and she's like, it's an in-depth training,

37:12.100 --> 37:15.780
but she, you know, she thinks
that it's good for that.

37:15.780 --> 37:19.540
And because just like anybody else,

37:19.540 --> 37:23.880
women are not treated right, either,

37:23.880 --> 37:27.100
whether they're in a heterosexual,
homosexual relationship

37:27.100 --> 37:31.780
and I'm starting to see
that people in my church,

37:31.780 --> 37:33.870
and I know that a lot of
feminists in my church,

37:33.870 --> 37:36.490
and they're letting me see
things in a different light.

37:36.490 --> 37:38.700
And I'm like, I'm glad
that they've done that

37:38.700 --> 37:41.160
because I didn't see it at that way.

37:41.160 --> 37:43.050
And they show me like, you know, Carl,

37:43.050 --> 37:44.230
this is really how you are.

37:44.230 --> 37:47.830
You're very oppressing your partner

37:47.830 --> 37:50.578
and you shouldn't do that.

37:50.578 --> 37:52.453
And then it's interesting.

37:53.910 --> 37:56.733
I see myself being an activist also.

37:59.422 --> 38:01.380
Starting in January, I'm with this,

38:01.380 --> 38:03.590
I'm gonna be a mentor, starting a group

38:03.590 --> 38:06.650
with African American, young trans men

38:06.650 --> 38:09.417
and working with two other people

38:09.417 --> 38:13.530
and just having a little
monthly group meeting with them

38:13.530 --> 38:17.703
and to mentor the young
guys and everything.

38:19.751 --> 38:24.751
And I wanna teach them how
to be a good young man.

38:25.170 --> 38:27.560
Being African-American, I wanna show

38:27.560 --> 38:29.623
a positive side to black men.

38:31.399 --> 38:33.960
There's so many stereotypical
things about black men

38:33.960 --> 38:38.030
that I don't like, and I know
I'm a positive black man.

38:38.030 --> 38:40.700
I'm married, I have kids, I work,

38:40.700 --> 38:44.550
and I'm a minister, I go to church.

38:44.550 --> 38:49.360
So I'm not thuggin' and a
buggin' and all this other stuff,

38:49.360 --> 38:52.120
trying to hustle, but
you know, I'm just here

38:52.120 --> 38:54.920
having a house, a home with my family.

38:54.920 --> 38:57.060
And I think that's a positive thing,

38:57.060 --> 38:59.643
but society has us as a negative.

39:01.297 --> 39:03.880
(upbeat music)

39:08.170 --> 39:09.860
- I wrote poetry to stay alive

39:09.860 --> 39:11.240
and I've always written to stay alive.

39:11.240 --> 39:13.610
I've either done that or music.

39:13.610 --> 39:15.950
And I really got hooked
onto Saul Williams.

39:15.950 --> 39:18.440
I saw the movie "Slam,"
and it ended it for me.

39:18.440 --> 39:20.280
Like I was writing this cheesy ass poetry,

39:20.280 --> 39:22.760
like the sun rises and stuff,

39:22.760 --> 39:25.480
and I saw "Slam," and
I was like, oh my God.

39:25.480 --> 39:30.480
And he will always be, he
will always be like my,

39:30.550 --> 39:34.830
you know, I hate idols, but
he will always be that guy

39:34.830 --> 39:37.310
who really pulled me out of myself.

39:37.310 --> 39:40.090
And then "Common" came on the scene for me

39:40.090 --> 39:41.290
and blew it out the box.

39:41.290 --> 39:44.783
And then Tylique, I met
him actually this summer.

39:45.890 --> 39:48.108
I mean, Mos Def, and all of these people

39:48.108 --> 39:52.830
who I had not listened to,
because I was in this utopia

39:52.830 --> 39:54.630
and lying to myself about my identity,

39:54.630 --> 39:56.513
chose not to listen to,
or to be involved in.

39:56.513 --> 39:59.160
And I, and I got into hip-hop
and I got into poetry,

39:59.160 --> 40:01.410
and it really just
changed my world in terms

40:01.410 --> 40:03.660
of me being comfortable
with my identity too.

40:08.360 --> 40:09.950
So that's how I started poetry,

40:09.950 --> 40:12.510
I wrote poetry all the time,
but it never really progressed

40:12.510 --> 40:15.470
to the point until I was at
a breaking point in my life

40:15.470 --> 40:20.180
where I could've lived or
died, you know, literally.

40:20.180 --> 40:21.151
So.

40:21.151 --> 40:23.734
(upbeat music)

40:26.280 --> 40:28.750
Kermit thought it wasn't easy being green.

40:28.750 --> 40:30.810
And so when his puppet
world of make believe,

40:30.810 --> 40:33.200
he believed in teaching
children on public television

40:33.200 --> 40:35.150
about racism, prejudice,

40:35.150 --> 40:37.480
and how really mean the world can be.

40:37.480 --> 40:38.920
He reached me through him melodic songs,

40:38.920 --> 40:41.240
teaching me my ABCs,
how to respect my toys,

40:41.240 --> 40:44.430
how not to hate boys,
and generally I received,

40:44.430 --> 40:46.630
I received in cars, parks,
my mother's basement,

40:46.630 --> 40:48.340
adjacent to where my childhood struggles

40:48.340 --> 40:51.810
always rustled my sheets,
the three Rs became regret,

40:51.810 --> 40:54.410
regression, reiterating
that it wasn't easy

40:54.410 --> 40:55.970
being brown either.

40:55.970 --> 40:57.360
Neither was being born into a system,

40:57.360 --> 40:58.555
made to keep my face unseen,

40:58.555 --> 41:00.520
wrinkles of scorn made to conform.

41:00.520 --> 41:01.980
I sit in my mother's computer swivel chair

41:01.980 --> 41:03.750
while she pressed my hair.

41:03.750 --> 41:06.610
Every Sunday, my mother never
washed my hair on Monday

41:06.610 --> 41:09.270
because Sunday was the Sabbath day.

41:09.270 --> 41:11.000
So I'm kinda skeptical of Christianity

41:11.000 --> 41:13.440
'cause if Jesus is really
white and heaven is forever,

41:13.440 --> 41:15.150
will I suffer in eternity?

41:15.150 --> 41:18.230
When I sit down here looking
up, it's His eyes down on me,

41:18.230 --> 41:20.430
down on myself, I used to
put razor blades to my arms

41:20.430 --> 41:23.090
as indigenous tribe markings
to keep you alarmed.

41:23.090 --> 41:25.070
Red and black, get back Jack,

41:25.070 --> 41:27.280
like The Scarlet Letter and
rules of the Boy Scouts,

41:27.280 --> 41:30.630
I grew stout, that was
that, but not like the PA's,

41:30.630 --> 41:33.200
but I will often enter the
offices with PhD markings.

41:33.200 --> 41:34.860
Doctors couldn't find what was wrong.

41:34.860 --> 41:36.910
Years were shorter instead of long.

41:36.910 --> 41:38.810
And I long for the moment
you where I would wake up

41:38.810 --> 41:40.750
like Luca with a struggle on my back.

41:40.750 --> 41:42.450
Instead of hunching it
over, letting it roll down,

41:42.450 --> 41:44.310
trying to keep my coloredness in the back.

41:44.310 --> 41:46.830
And I'd often retract, taking
seats in the back of buses.

41:46.830 --> 41:48.560
It's always warmer there.

41:48.560 --> 41:50.260
And no matter how often people stare,

41:50.260 --> 41:53.367
I hum a song, my ancestors' song.

41:53.367 --> 41:56.640
♪ Swing low, sweet chariot ♪

41:56.640 --> 41:58.640
but this bus isn't taking me home.

41:58.640 --> 41:59.720
Underground, the mini railroads,

41:59.720 --> 42:01.497
I found and regain this
freedom and this time

42:01.497 --> 42:03.460
I will not follow the drinking gourd,

42:03.460 --> 42:05.930
'cause if I consume on an
ounce of liquid, I converse,

42:05.930 --> 42:07.940
my father's father's
father's fathers had seen

42:07.940 --> 42:08.960
too much water in their lifetimes,

42:08.960 --> 42:10.410
so I ignore my thirst.

42:10.410 --> 42:12.280
I wanna go back to the time of my birth,

42:12.280 --> 42:14.630
where I shaped my mother's
belly with psychotic telepathy,

42:14.630 --> 42:18.114
consisting of only cells instead
of existing in this jail.

42:18.114 --> 42:21.920
Only time will tell, but
it's already been 400 years,

42:21.920 --> 42:25.330
428 years, and I still can't
see past my broad nose.

42:25.330 --> 42:28.830
And I only get 28 days in
February to say it loud.

42:28.830 --> 42:31.910
I'm black and I'm proud,
but it takes a little longer

42:31.910 --> 42:33.600
to fake it till you make it.

42:33.600 --> 42:34.980
Reality hit me hard.

42:34.980 --> 42:37.573
Like Adam and Eve,
realizing they were naked.

42:39.460 --> 42:41.930
Even before I could spell, I was writing,

42:41.930 --> 42:44.830
even before I knew my
letters, I was writing.

42:44.830 --> 42:47.619
It sounds so cheesy, but
you could ask my mom,

42:47.619 --> 42:50.990
I would scribble some crap down
and be like, dah, dah, dah.

42:50.990 --> 42:52.973
So I've always been a writer,

42:54.909 --> 42:57.580
and that's just my passion,
that's what I wanna do.

42:57.580 --> 42:59.593
When I get, when I got into college,

43:01.840 --> 43:03.850
I went to go get a checkup or something.

43:03.850 --> 43:07.270
And the doctor's like, you
know, noticed something.

43:07.270 --> 43:09.200
And I was like, whoa.

43:09.200 --> 43:11.197
And that's when it kinda hit me.

43:11.197 --> 43:13.647
And so I went back and I
did a bunch of research.

43:14.790 --> 43:19.790
And then it came out that
I had a really severe case

43:20.190 --> 43:24.430
of polycystic ovary syndrome,
which physically changed

43:24.430 --> 43:29.167
my body and made it
difficult for me to produce

43:31.600 --> 43:33.410
the right amount of estrogen.

43:33.410 --> 43:36.690
And I went back to the
doctor because my weight

43:36.690 --> 43:38.230
started to gain again.

43:38.230 --> 43:39.890
'Cause they took me
off all the medication,

43:39.890 --> 43:41.960
they're like, you're fine,
and then I called the doctor,

43:41.960 --> 43:43.120
and I'm like, what's going on?

43:43.120 --> 43:44.190
And he's like, this is something

43:44.190 --> 43:45.330
that you'll always have to deal with.

43:45.330 --> 43:46.520
There is no cure.

43:46.520 --> 43:48.654
And sometimes your medication
is not going to work,

43:48.654 --> 43:51.520
but because I was so
unhealthy and so overweight,

43:51.520 --> 43:55.950
I was just terrified of always
having to be on a drug and,

43:55.950 --> 43:58.360
and never being really
able to maintain my weight

43:58.360 --> 44:00.490
or my emotions or my moods.

44:00.490 --> 44:04.223
And so after that, I kinda was like,

44:05.080 --> 44:06.160
I fell in love with this girl.

44:06.160 --> 44:08.060
And I was just so comfortable with myself.

44:08.060 --> 44:10.300
And, and then I kind of woke
up one day and was like,

44:10.300 --> 44:12.203
man, I think I'm trans.

44:13.320 --> 44:16.290
I was one of those trans
guys was like, you know,

44:16.290 --> 44:19.010
I was kind of like that
jock who's homophobic.

44:19.010 --> 44:22.430
I was super femme, and I
didn't really, you know,

44:22.430 --> 44:24.560
after I got into Yellowstone,
which is the treatment center,

44:24.560 --> 44:27.130
I was just like, why do
girls dress like guys?

44:27.130 --> 44:30.820
And I was just really
trying to, in my mind,

44:30.820 --> 44:33.400
rationalize or try to piece together

44:33.400 --> 44:35.430
what was going on with my body,

44:35.430 --> 44:37.460
in my mind, putting those together.

44:37.460 --> 44:41.890
And then I just let all those,
I was just like, forget it.

44:41.890 --> 44:45.610
And I just, it, it was
really kinda like I woke up

44:45.610 --> 44:46.690
one day and I was like, I was trans.

44:46.690 --> 44:49.607
I knew all along, clearly, the
signs were there, you know?

44:49.607 --> 44:52.557
And when I look back, I'm like,
wow, you know, I was trans.

44:53.630 --> 44:56.699
But I really literally woke up one day

44:56.699 --> 45:01.699
and told my girlfriend
that I thought I was trans.

45:02.010 --> 45:03.200
And that was it.

45:03.200 --> 45:04.840
Before I transitioned, I was like, well,

45:04.840 --> 45:06.060
just everyone call me Jim.

45:06.060 --> 45:08.743
And I was like, Jim's kinda
a white boy's name, you know?

45:08.743 --> 45:10.130
And I was like, I don't know,

45:10.130 --> 45:12.480
And my friend was like,
let's, you know, Jay Bird.

45:12.480 --> 45:14.680
And I was like, okay,
you can just call me Jay.

45:14.680 --> 45:16.240
And so then Jay kind of stuck.

45:16.240 --> 45:19.713
And I thought Jay was kind
of unisex because if I, my,

45:20.740 --> 45:23.040
my ID hadn't been changed and
I hadn't started hormones.

45:23.040 --> 45:25.440
And I was, I did have
kind of feminine features.

45:25.440 --> 45:28.157
So it was a unisex name that I could use

45:28.157 --> 45:29.580
and people wouldn't be like, James,

45:29.580 --> 45:30.880
and you're like, you know?

45:32.300 --> 45:34.643
So I that's what I did.

45:35.870 --> 45:40.290
I did keep my middle name close
to my original middle name,

45:40.290 --> 45:42.600
which was Nicole, so now it's Nicholas,

45:42.600 --> 45:44.670
because I didn't want
my parents to, you know,

45:44.670 --> 45:46.470
I wanted to keep something.

45:46.470 --> 45:48.190
'Cause I think that they, that parents

45:48.190 --> 45:50.008
get so attached to the name, you know?

45:50.008 --> 45:52.310
'Cause children, it's special
and I had a unique name,

45:52.310 --> 45:56.920
sort of, it's kind of popular
now, but Christmas this year,

45:56.920 --> 45:58.883
my name James was on my packages.

45:59.980 --> 46:02.461
They wrote on the calendar
when I was coming home, Jay,

46:02.461 --> 46:03.294
you know what I'm saying?

46:03.294 --> 46:05.447
So they know that's what, where it's at,

46:05.447 --> 46:07.730
and when I'm in public, they really try.

46:07.730 --> 46:11.090
'Cause now people are starting
to look at them crazy,

46:11.090 --> 46:13.750
they're like Katie, you know, come here.

46:13.750 --> 46:15.610
And they're like, what?

46:15.610 --> 46:19.460
So I mean, they're really,
my dad is really good.

46:19.460 --> 46:21.560
My dad has taken it the best.

46:21.560 --> 46:24.640
He put my ass in
construction and it's like,

46:24.640 --> 46:26.750
you're gonna learn a trade, and
if you're going to be a man,

46:26.750 --> 46:29.420
you know, he helped, you know,
he buys me clothes that fit.

46:29.420 --> 46:32.330
And because really, I don't
know how to dress myself.

46:32.330 --> 46:35.030
I mean, I can, I'm starting
to get to the point

46:35.030 --> 46:39.130
where I'm working a 9 to 5
job and I need to wear clothes

46:39.130 --> 46:44.130
that are kind of casual,
but you know, work casual.

46:44.720 --> 46:46.734
And so he really helps me out with that.

46:46.734 --> 46:49.530
Haircuts, you know, telling
me how to cut my hair

46:49.530 --> 46:50.870
so I don't look like a little boy,

46:50.870 --> 46:53.310
'cause I do look young
and just things like that.

46:53.310 --> 46:57.103
So my dad's been really,
really good about,

46:58.340 --> 46:59.370
he's kind of stereotypical.

46:59.370 --> 47:00.990
Like I said, you put me in construction,

47:00.990 --> 47:04.850
you need to learn how to
paint some drywall, you know,

47:04.850 --> 47:06.380
and saw this plank of wood.

47:06.380 --> 47:08.470
But that's what he knows.

47:08.470 --> 47:11.170
And that's great because
I think that there's a lot

47:11.170 --> 47:13.140
of bio men who don't have fathers either.

47:13.140 --> 47:15.050
And I was just blessed to
be able to have a father.

47:15.050 --> 47:19.280
My mom, however, I'm her little
girl and I'm the youngest.

47:19.280 --> 47:20.620
So it's immensely hard.

47:20.620 --> 47:22.670
Like my sister, she prides herself

47:22.670 --> 47:24.070
on having a little sister and my mom

47:24.070 --> 47:26.430
was like my baby, my little baby girl.

47:26.430 --> 47:29.023
And that's what she calls
me and I don't press it.

47:30.850 --> 47:33.633
I'm not a person who
really cares about that.

47:33.633 --> 47:34.740
I just want my parents to love me.

47:34.740 --> 47:36.863
They didn't kick me
out, they're supportive,

47:38.110 --> 47:43.110
and that's all I could
ask for really, you know,

47:43.308 --> 47:46.580
and they don't sweep it under
the rug now like they used to.

47:46.580 --> 47:47.660
They're really coming around,

47:47.660 --> 47:50.080
especially since I'm physically changing.

47:50.080 --> 47:51.390
My dad looked at me one day and he's like,

47:51.390 --> 47:53.960
wow, you're, you're
really looking different.

47:53.960 --> 47:56.910
And so I think tangibly,
when they, when they saw it,

47:56.910 --> 47:58.880
then they were more accepting of the fact

47:58.880 --> 48:01.420
that this is what it was, you know?

48:01.420 --> 48:04.570
I'm still psychologically
going through puberty,

48:04.570 --> 48:06.289
even though I'm grown.

48:06.289 --> 48:10.730
I'm 22 and I'm grown,
I mean, right? (laughs)

48:10.730 --> 48:14.280
No, but I'm I'm older,
you know what I'm saying?

48:14.280 --> 48:17.640
And it's so weird that I'm
like, I wanna look like 50 Cent,

48:17.640 --> 48:22.640
you know, or just, you know,
my physical, you know, image.

48:23.240 --> 48:24.912
I don't care to be a gangster.

48:24.912 --> 48:26.140
I don't really do that.

48:26.140 --> 48:28.080
I don't care to be, which is a lot

48:28.080 --> 48:31.287
of the media portrayal of
black man is that they're hard.

48:31.287 --> 48:32.900
And I don't care anything about that.

48:32.900 --> 48:34.100
'Cause I'm a poet, you know?

48:34.100 --> 48:35.920
So when you go into the poetry scene,

48:35.920 --> 48:37.690
you've got these like cool black dudes

48:37.690 --> 48:39.812
who are just like chill, it's whatever.

48:39.812 --> 48:44.610
So I'm not really concerned
with that so much.

48:44.610 --> 48:46.030
I think it's just physically,

48:46.030 --> 48:48.050
I'm just like, I wanna
be, yeah. I wanna look

48:48.050 --> 48:51.490
kind of like that because
I think I'm still early.

48:51.490 --> 48:53.290
I've only been on testosterone for a year,

48:53.290 --> 48:55.999
so I'm still early in like
my physical transformation.

48:55.999 --> 48:58.590
So I'm like, oh, I kinda want
to have a body like that.

48:58.590 --> 49:00.453
Or I want my voice to be like that.

49:06.060 --> 49:08.170
I think that other people expect me

49:08.170 --> 49:10.980
to be what the media portrays of black men

49:13.232 --> 49:14.703
and that's difficult.

49:18.310 --> 49:19.460
But I struggled with that

49:19.460 --> 49:23.120
in my cheese mode crap all the time.

49:23.120 --> 49:25.370
And I still struggle with it in a sense

49:25.370 --> 49:29.350
that now I question my
own manner to my own,

49:29.350 --> 49:31.660
not to project it so much outwardly,

49:31.660 --> 49:33.263
but I wanna feel it inside.

49:34.140 --> 49:35.740
I don't want to wear this sign of my head

49:35.740 --> 49:37.230
that I'm trans 'cause I'm a man.

49:37.230 --> 49:39.370
Now I don't, I just
want to be with a woman.

49:39.370 --> 49:42.773
And ironically, when I'm down South,

49:43.980 --> 49:45.860
a lot of my female coworkers know,

49:45.860 --> 49:47.980
and they're like, all right, cool.

49:47.980 --> 49:50.170
You know, it doesn't dawn, it's just,

49:50.170 --> 49:55.080
they don't care at all,
and so it's just like...

49:57.710 --> 49:58.543
Weird.

49:58.543 --> 49:59.376
It's so weird.

50:00.591 --> 50:03.206
And up here, they care a little bit more.

50:03.206 --> 50:06.290
I think they care little bit
more, just in my observation,

50:06.290 --> 50:09.183
but down there, they're
just like, that is so sexy,

50:09.183 --> 50:10.016
because here's the thing,

50:10.016 --> 50:12.150
is that it's so patriarchal down there.

50:12.150 --> 50:14.690
It's like women have to
wait on men, hand and foot.

50:14.690 --> 50:19.270
And so here's this man coming
in with this feminine side,

50:19.270 --> 50:21.400
you know, this femininity that is weird,

50:21.400 --> 50:24.700
but still has this kind of
macho machismo thing going on,

50:24.700 --> 50:26.887
but it's not overbearing where it's like,

50:26.887 --> 50:30.270
they're in this form of servitude, almost.

50:30.270 --> 50:32.110
They call it something else there,

50:32.110 --> 50:34.350
what is it, chivalry or whatever.

50:34.350 --> 50:35.883
Which I think is crap.

50:37.433 --> 50:38.580
Will you hold my purse?

50:38.580 --> 50:39.490
It's heavy.

50:39.490 --> 50:43.510
But I think that they like the simple,

50:43.510 --> 50:47.520
the fact that I can be, I hate that both,

50:47.520 --> 50:51.660
but I possess kind of two halves,

50:51.660 --> 50:54.010
because I'm understanding
their feminine experience,

50:54.010 --> 50:58.226
but also able to be a
man, you know, be a man.

50:58.226 --> 51:00.809
(upbeat music)

51:07.410 --> 51:09.593
- Early on in my life,

51:11.316 --> 51:13.710
I think when I was young adult,
it was before I got sober.

51:13.710 --> 51:17.210
I had hitchhiked to Texas and
was being just, you know, bad.

51:17.210 --> 51:20.200
And I met these drag queens
who were taking hormones

51:20.200 --> 51:23.970
and I had this fantasy that someday

51:23.970 --> 51:25.570
I would take hormones and be a man.

51:25.570 --> 51:28.283
I had no idea that it was
possible for one thing,

51:29.640 --> 51:30.880
but that was my fantasy.

51:30.880 --> 51:33.850
And when I met the first couple of guys

51:33.850 --> 51:36.503
in the Bay Area that were transitioning,

51:37.639 --> 51:39.390
it was the first time that I realized

51:39.390 --> 51:42.740
that it could be something
other than a fantasy.

51:42.740 --> 51:43.760
And it was hard.

51:43.760 --> 51:46.793
I mean, 'cause I think
more than anything else,

51:48.380 --> 51:50.150
I was scared to tell my mom, you know,

51:50.150 --> 51:53.330
and I was afraid to face her disapproval.

51:53.330 --> 51:57.850
And I think for a period of time,

51:57.850 --> 52:02.440
I did nothing because I was
afraid of her disapproval.

52:02.440 --> 52:04.980
And then I realized at a certain point

52:04.980 --> 52:06.960
that I was waiting for her to die,

52:06.960 --> 52:09.900
so I could actually live my
life, and that really felt

52:09.900 --> 52:14.900
horrible and morbid to me,
and I decided to move forward,

52:16.510 --> 52:18.780
I took my first shot on my birthday,

52:18.780 --> 52:22.890
and I was so grateful that
my period stopped right away.

52:22.890 --> 52:25.520
That that alone was worth
taking that first shot.

52:25.520 --> 52:28.260
'Cause man feeling like a guy

52:28.260 --> 52:29.770
and having a period just sucks.

52:29.770 --> 52:31.830
It just is such a bad experience

52:31.830 --> 52:34.100
psychologically and emotionally.

52:34.100 --> 52:36.160
And then, you know, I went through

52:36.160 --> 52:38.840
that kind of Peter Brady moment
when my voice was changing

52:38.840 --> 52:43.840
and that was really weird, and
then like hair growth hurts.

52:44.210 --> 52:46.160
I didn't, nobody, I called my brother.

52:46.160 --> 52:47.777
And I said, "Dude, why didn't you tell me

52:47.777 --> 52:49.197
"that this was gonna like hurt,

52:49.197 --> 52:51.430
"and I was going to get
these bumps and be itchy?"

52:51.430 --> 52:53.640
And he said, "Oh, I
figured you'd find out."

52:53.640 --> 52:56.270
I mean, he just, he's a joker.

52:56.270 --> 52:57.280
We get along really well.

52:57.280 --> 52:59.930
But so I mean, that was,
it was interesting to watch

52:59.930 --> 53:04.090
my body change and watch my,
listen to my voice change

53:04.090 --> 53:04.923
and things like that.

53:04.923 --> 53:06.800
And I think in my relationship

53:06.800 --> 53:11.397
there was some difficulty
because neither my wife nor I,

53:11.397 --> 53:15.150
and she wasn't my wife, then,
she was my fiancee then,

53:15.150 --> 53:17.680
we weren't sure that she
would still be attracted to me

53:17.680 --> 53:19.647
the more that I masculinized,

53:19.647 --> 53:23.210
and so that was just really
kind of a wait and see thing,

53:23.210 --> 53:27.720
which was scary, but we had
integrity with each other

53:27.720 --> 53:30.600
about it and it's all worked out well,

53:30.600 --> 53:33.518
but you know, it was, I think
a lot of relationships end

53:33.518 --> 53:36.920
not because there isn't love there,

53:36.920 --> 53:39.770
but for people who are lesbian identified,

53:39.770 --> 53:42.359
even if they're attracted
to butch lesbians,

53:42.359 --> 53:44.230
there's something about being with a man

53:44.230 --> 53:46.580
that is just not
compelling to them at all.

53:46.580 --> 53:49.660
And so you have to honor
that and respect that,

53:49.660 --> 53:52.040
but I got lucky.

53:52.040 --> 53:53.790
I mean, that's all I
can say is I got lucky,

53:53.790 --> 53:57.070
but it's been interesting.

53:57.070 --> 53:59.560
Not because of the transition.

53:59.560 --> 54:04.560
I think in her family,
the idea that she would be

54:05.860 --> 54:07.310
with someone who's African-American

54:07.310 --> 54:09.370
is just abhorrent to them.

54:09.370 --> 54:14.370
She's a mixed race woman,
European-American, and Peruvian.

54:15.040 --> 54:18.520
And I think there's, there's
a certain amount of bigotry

54:18.520 --> 54:22.400
in both sides of her
family line and the notion

54:22.400 --> 54:24.160
that is true often in the black community

54:24.160 --> 54:26.250
and other communities of color,

54:26.250 --> 54:28.930
that you should marry white
to kind of better the race

54:28.930 --> 54:31.100
and preferably someone
of the opposite sex,

54:31.100 --> 54:34.110
so that she brought home
this black trans man

54:34.110 --> 54:37.450
was just not a fun experience.

54:37.450 --> 54:41.520
And her ex, in a fit of pique,
actually emailed her parents

54:41.520 --> 54:43.170
and told them that I was trans.

54:43.170 --> 54:45.560
So I didn't really get
to come out to them.

54:45.560 --> 54:46.450
They already knew.

54:46.450 --> 54:49.220
So there was all this buzz
about it, that was me,

54:49.220 --> 54:52.930
I was it, this thing that she was seeing

54:52.930 --> 54:57.930
and in our relationship, I think
because of that experience,

54:58.370 --> 55:00.620
we were forced to kind
of stand together more

55:03.030 --> 55:07.240
and she's just been, just,

55:07.240 --> 55:08.540
probably the greatest gift of my life.

55:08.540 --> 55:12.160
I mean, she's been,
she's pushed me at times

55:12.160 --> 55:15.364
when I needed to be
pushed and has needled me

55:15.364 --> 55:19.890
and pushed me to be a better
person and a stronger person.

55:19.890 --> 55:21.360
And she's been incredibly supportive.

55:21.360 --> 55:25.638
And for her, it's been very
hard because as as a femme woman

55:25.638 --> 55:28.630
and as a mixed race woman,
she's had a hard time

55:28.630 --> 55:29.680
with her own visibility.

55:29.680 --> 55:32.630
So as a mixed race woman, who's very pale,

55:32.630 --> 55:35.230
she's often mistaken for white.

55:35.230 --> 55:38.830
As a femme woman, the only
way she was kind of identified

55:38.830 --> 55:42.190
as a femme lesbian was
with her butch on her arm.

55:42.190 --> 55:45.410
And so now she's with
this black man in this,

55:45.410 --> 55:48.440
what looks to be straight
married relationship.

55:48.440 --> 55:52.400
And it just, I've been
very thoughtful about her,

55:52.400 --> 55:55.480
the visibility that she's
lost by being with me,

55:55.480 --> 55:57.410
and we've had a lot of discussion about it

55:57.410 --> 55:59.380
and she's been really great.

55:59.380 --> 56:01.430
And so I don't, she has
my permission to out me

56:01.430 --> 56:04.460
wherever she wants to and
wherever it feels comfortable

56:04.460 --> 56:07.123
for her, so that she can
maintain her own integrity.

56:08.700 --> 56:11.430
My wife and I do GLBT diversity trainings

56:11.430 --> 56:14.640
for different organizations,
and it's really kinda fun

56:14.640 --> 56:16.750
because we do a three day training.

56:16.750 --> 56:18.660
The first day is really about,

56:18.660 --> 56:20.930
kind of stereotypes and labels.

56:20.930 --> 56:23.840
And we go through all these
different ways in which people

56:23.840 --> 56:28.763
identify people in the
LGBTQQI communities.

56:30.298 --> 56:32.430
And we spend a lot of
time talking about that,

56:32.430 --> 56:36.328
about people's perceptions,
people's difficulty

56:36.328 --> 56:40.010
trying to intersect the work
that they're trying to do

56:40.010 --> 56:42.222
in human services or in business

56:42.222 --> 56:44.640
and their own kind of maybe spiritual

56:44.640 --> 56:48.120
or religious positions
on what it means to be

56:48.120 --> 56:50.320
in a same sex involvement.

56:50.320 --> 56:53.253
And we just kinda let that
flesh itself out a bit.

56:55.060 --> 56:57.320
The second day, we come out.

56:57.320 --> 57:00.173
So what we look like is
we look like, perhaps,

57:01.116 --> 57:05.500
a married couple of
advocates and activists,

57:05.500 --> 57:09.460
and then my wife comes out
as being lesbian identified,

57:09.460 --> 57:10.870
and I come out as being a trans man,

57:10.870 --> 57:14.460
and we spend time answering a
bunch of questions about that

57:14.460 --> 57:17.520
and talking about the
processes of transition,

57:17.520 --> 57:20.220
because that's what people
are very interested in.

57:20.220 --> 57:23.500
When I do come out to folks,
I think they're surprised.

57:23.500 --> 57:25.890
I haven't had anybody respond negatively,

57:25.890 --> 57:27.920
but I think they're more kind of surprised

57:27.920 --> 57:31.560
and a little bit in shock, excuse me.

57:31.560 --> 57:36.020
And they don't, if they
feel negatively about it,

57:36.020 --> 57:37.670
they haven't bothered to tell me.

57:38.640 --> 57:40.440
It's been, you know, I mean, I think

57:41.610 --> 57:44.560
in the, in the butch lesbian
community, there was a lot of,

57:45.690 --> 57:49.090
I think that was the, the, my
friends who identify as butch

57:49.090 --> 57:51.350
probably gave me the hardest
time about transition.

57:51.350 --> 57:54.710
And I think going from
being kind of an elder

57:54.710 --> 57:59.710
in that community to being a
guy was very hard for folks,

57:59.960 --> 58:02.263
you know, and I also think that,

58:04.200 --> 58:07.060
you know, you go from being
an ally and a strength

58:07.060 --> 58:09.880
and someone that people can
turn to in that community

58:09.880 --> 58:12.090
to being, you know, the
face of their perpetrator

58:12.090 --> 58:13.020
and their oppressor.

58:13.020 --> 58:17.000
And so, I understand
some of that difficulty.

58:17.000 --> 58:21.100
It was hard, it was hurtful,
but I also understand it.

58:21.100 --> 58:26.100
And I took what I call
my, my farewell tour

58:26.790 --> 58:28.220
the year before I transitioned.

58:28.220 --> 58:30.620
And I spent a year really going to all

58:30.620 --> 58:32.610
the women's only spaces
that I would never be able

58:32.610 --> 58:36.850
to visit again, and to just
say, thank you, and to be,

58:36.850 --> 58:38.890
to express gratitude for the nurturing

58:38.890 --> 58:40.940
that came out of the lesbian community,

58:40.940 --> 58:43.190
that allowed me to be
kind of true to myself.

58:46.290 --> 58:47.170
And I miss it sometimes.

58:47.170 --> 58:48.910
I mean, even now, if we go out to a bar,

58:48.910 --> 58:51.940
a lesbian bar or something,
I just feel so weird.

58:51.940 --> 58:54.360
I mean, 'cause all, I'm
looking at these, like I said,

58:54.360 --> 58:57.100
I don't really see myself
very differently, really.

58:57.100 --> 58:59.220
And so I'm looking at folks
and going, "Hey, what's up?"

58:59.220 --> 59:01.530
And they're looking at me like,
what is this guy doing here?

59:01.530 --> 59:03.262
You know, what does he want?

59:03.262 --> 59:06.713
And I realized that I've
traded the opportunity

59:08.920 --> 59:11.520
to be a part of this community

59:11.520 --> 59:14.490
for whatever male privilege
it is I have out in the world

59:14.490 --> 59:18.960
and I wouldn't untrade it,
but it's sometimes difficult

59:18.960 --> 59:20.373
and sometimes uncomfortable.

59:22.230 --> 59:24.780
I'm not sure that it's
affected my masculinity now

59:24.780 --> 59:28.940
as much as it did earlier
in my life as a butch,

59:28.940 --> 59:31.290
and earlier on in transition

59:31.290 --> 59:32.620
because there's that feeling of feeling

59:32.620 --> 59:36.500
like I had to overcompensate
because I was female bodied

59:36.500 --> 59:39.470
and that was more true as a butch.

59:39.470 --> 59:42.610
I think, you know, I couldn't,
I had to be harder than hard

59:42.610 --> 59:44.800
and tougher than tough and I wouldn't cry,

59:44.800 --> 59:46.723
and you know, all that stuff that,

59:48.480 --> 59:51.530
I had to do all those things
because I wasn't a man.

59:51.530 --> 59:54.200
And I think in transition, I realized

59:55.700 --> 59:57.170
I don't have to do anything.

59:57.170 --> 59:59.150
I can be whatever kind of man I wanna be

59:59.150 --> 01:00:01.750
because now I'm a man,
you know, now it fits.

01:00:01.750 --> 01:00:04.000
And I don't think I realized

01:00:04.000 --> 01:00:06.550
that I was overcompensating at the time.

01:00:06.550 --> 01:00:10.500
I think I was trying to insulate myself

01:00:10.500 --> 01:00:15.033
against not being male enough.

01:00:16.020 --> 01:00:17.230
I don't know, I really don't think

01:00:17.230 --> 01:00:18.840
it was on a conscious level,

01:00:18.840 --> 01:00:21.010
but there was something
like a sigh of relief

01:00:21.010 --> 01:00:24.160
that happened when I,
when I became integrated,

01:00:24.160 --> 01:00:25.610
my insides and my outsides.

01:00:25.610 --> 01:00:28.800
And I realized that I can be soft,

01:00:28.800 --> 01:00:31.030
and I can be goofy and I can, you know,

01:00:31.030 --> 01:00:34.100
I can do whatever I wanna
do because this is who I am.

01:00:34.100 --> 01:00:36.270
I don't have to prove anything to anybody.

01:00:36.270 --> 01:00:39.979
So the one thing that really
I think is most telling

01:00:39.979 --> 01:00:44.260
is that I'm now beginning a relationship

01:00:44.260 --> 01:00:46.590
with my body after so
many years of pretending

01:00:46.590 --> 01:00:48.380
that it wasn't there.

01:00:48.380 --> 01:00:52.880
And so I didn't, I wouldn't
work out, I wouldn't eat right.

01:00:52.880 --> 01:00:55.320
I wouldn't take care of my
body because I hated my body.

01:00:55.320 --> 01:00:56.260
I didn't want it, you know?

01:00:56.260 --> 01:01:00.440
I wanted it to go away
and now I'm kinda working

01:01:00.440 --> 01:01:02.120
my way back from that of going, okay,

01:01:02.120 --> 01:01:03.450
I've got the body I want now.

01:01:03.450 --> 01:01:06.270
Let's see if we can't give it
a little love and attention,

01:01:06.270 --> 01:01:09.510
but I think more than anything
else, the greatest impact

01:01:09.510 --> 01:01:11.860
was really a divorce
from my physical self.

01:01:11.860 --> 01:01:15.470
Like from all those years, even sexually,

01:01:15.470 --> 01:01:18.923
I was not willing to be
attached to my physical body.

01:01:22.900 --> 01:01:25.550
I'm fortunate enough to
be associated with folks

01:01:25.550 --> 01:01:27.360
that are earlier in their transition.

01:01:27.360 --> 01:01:29.890
And I encourage them to
take a farewell tour,

01:01:29.890 --> 01:01:34.190
you know, take that year and,
and to integrate who you were

01:01:34.190 --> 01:01:36.920
with who you're becoming,
because it's all you.

01:01:36.920 --> 01:01:38.630
You don't have to flush
away that part of it,

01:01:38.630 --> 01:01:41.380
that's part of what will make
you the man that you are.

01:01:42.420 --> 01:01:47.420
And I would also say that
it's been important for me

01:01:47.490 --> 01:01:49.780
to recognize that my spiritual transition

01:01:49.780 --> 01:01:52.576
needed to precede my physical transition,

01:01:52.576 --> 01:01:55.900
that the physical transition
without the spiritual

01:01:55.900 --> 01:01:58.393
is not fulfilling and is kind of,

01:01:59.660 --> 01:02:04.590
perhaps leaning towards
problems down the road

01:02:04.590 --> 01:02:08.430
in terms of self acceptance
and acceptance in the world.

01:02:08.430 --> 01:02:10.900
And I would say again, give the people

01:02:10.900 --> 01:02:14.310
that you are causing
to transition with you

01:02:14.310 --> 01:02:15.570
at least as much time

01:02:15.570 --> 01:02:17.370
as it took you to find self-acceptance.

01:02:17.370 --> 01:02:20.320
Don't be impatient with your
parents and your siblings

01:02:20.320 --> 01:02:23.150
and all those folks
'cause they don't get it.

01:02:23.150 --> 01:02:24.193
It's not theirs to get, you know?

01:02:24.193 --> 01:02:27.130
I mean, we're in a position
when we transition,

01:02:27.130 --> 01:02:29.170
we force people to transition with us.

01:02:29.170 --> 01:02:30.880
Our loved ones have no choice.

01:02:30.880 --> 01:02:33.540
They can either do it gracefully or not,

01:02:33.540 --> 01:02:37.070
but we're moving on and
they have to kind of

01:02:37.070 --> 01:02:38.390
get dragged along with us.

01:02:38.390 --> 01:02:41.350
And so, sometimes that feels

01:02:41.350 --> 01:02:43.040
exactly like what's happening to them.

01:02:43.040 --> 01:02:44.940
They're getting dragged
along like a hostage.

01:02:44.940 --> 01:02:47.520
And we have to try to make room for them

01:02:47.520 --> 01:02:48.960
to wrestle with their own stuff.

01:02:48.960 --> 01:02:53.170
And in the ways in which
I've found the most loving

01:02:53.170 --> 01:02:56.840
to do that is to just be
present and to be whole,

01:02:56.840 --> 01:02:59.200
and to be pleased with who I am

01:02:59.200 --> 01:03:02.700
and to not be argumentative or defensive,

01:03:02.700 --> 01:03:04.927
but to say, "I completely understand

01:03:04.927 --> 01:03:07.047
"how you don't understand this.

01:03:07.047 --> 01:03:08.847
"I wasn't fortunate enough to be born

01:03:08.847 --> 01:03:10.167
"in the body that fit me.

01:03:10.167 --> 01:03:13.660
"But if I had, maybe I
wouldn't understand it either,"

01:03:13.660 --> 01:03:16.300
you know, give them
room to not understand.

01:03:16.300 --> 01:03:20.300
So that's really, and live,
you know, live and love boldly.

01:03:20.300 --> 01:03:21.710
That's what I would say, you know?

01:03:21.710 --> 01:03:23.860
Find someone that loves
you for who you are,

01:03:23.860 --> 01:03:28.030
all of who you are and
don't allow yourself

01:03:28.030 --> 01:03:29.670
to be disrespected.

01:03:29.670 --> 01:03:31.300
Even if it gets lonely, sometimes,

01:03:31.300 --> 01:03:34.103
waiting for someone that can
respect you, it's worth it.

01:03:54.461 --> 01:03:57.044
(upbeat music)

01:04:00.430 --> 01:04:04.170
- When I was in Vegas, I grew up.

01:04:04.170 --> 01:04:09.170
I mean, really I grew up
spiritually, mentally,

01:04:09.960 --> 01:04:14.960
emotionally, and everything.

01:04:15.261 --> 01:04:17.530
I didn't see my mom and my family,

01:04:17.530 --> 01:04:20.570
and those, in some years and every time

01:04:20.570 --> 01:04:22.730
they would send me pictures,

01:04:22.730 --> 01:04:25.153
I was noticing my family
was getting older.

01:04:27.100 --> 01:04:29.163
You know, and I'm like, wow.

01:04:30.090 --> 01:04:32.430
You know, there's some nephews and nieces

01:04:32.430 --> 01:04:33.920
that I haven't seen.

01:04:33.920 --> 01:04:36.460
And my mom was getting older.

01:04:36.460 --> 01:04:38.600
I was getting older and I was, you know,

01:04:38.600 --> 01:04:41.940
just ready to retire,
basically just start over.

01:04:41.940 --> 01:04:45.433
And so, because I haven't seen my family,

01:04:47.036 --> 01:04:48.880
and at that time, too, in 2002,

01:04:53.090 --> 01:04:58.070
I realized that, you know, my, who I was,

01:04:58.070 --> 01:05:00.863
and that's when I first started
transitioning, actually,

01:05:02.333 --> 01:05:05.666
2002, and I said, if I wanna transition,

01:05:07.499 --> 01:05:10.743
I wanna do it in, I wanna
go back home and do it.

01:05:11.660 --> 01:05:13.773
You know, I wanna go somewhere where,

01:05:16.590 --> 01:05:18.560
I feel safe, I would just feel safe,

01:05:18.560 --> 01:05:21.590
instead of transitioning out
there without any support.

01:05:21.590 --> 01:05:24.940
And not saying my family
was supportive, but just,

01:05:24.940 --> 01:05:27.783
you know, I don't know,
just support, you know.

01:05:30.160 --> 01:05:31.840
Like if anything happened
to me in an operation

01:05:31.840 --> 01:05:33.710
or something, you know, I'll
be there with my family,

01:05:33.710 --> 01:05:34.960
just something like that.

01:05:37.380 --> 01:05:39.470
But before I was able to
come here, I, you know,

01:05:39.470 --> 01:05:40.740
I had to write the letters.

01:05:40.740 --> 01:05:44.440
I had to tell them, you know,
that I'm about to transition.

01:05:44.440 --> 01:05:46.880
So I'm just, because they
haven't seen me in years,

01:05:46.880 --> 01:05:48.308
I just don't want to just go out there,

01:05:48.308 --> 01:05:51.520
and just say, "Hey," 'cause I was always,

01:05:51.520 --> 01:05:53.540
I still always kind of kept in contact

01:05:53.540 --> 01:05:54.820
with my family all the time,

01:05:54.820 --> 01:05:56.640
talk to my mom all the time.

01:05:56.640 --> 01:05:57.787
So I was calling them up and like,

01:05:57.787 --> 01:05:59.610
"Yeah, I'm changing my name."

01:05:59.610 --> 01:06:01.000
And they was like, yeah.

01:06:01.000 --> 01:06:03.800
'Cause you know, I was the type of person,

01:06:03.800 --> 01:06:06.090
I was the quote-unquote "gypsy."

01:06:06.090 --> 01:06:09.323
So whatever, any changes I
did, it was like, oh, okay.

01:06:10.240 --> 01:06:15.240
And then they made it okay
for me to be who I was.

01:06:15.460 --> 01:06:18.920
And as growing up as a child,
you want your family to say,

01:06:18.920 --> 01:06:21.260
okay, 'cause it was never about,

01:06:21.260 --> 01:06:24.200
it was never about the friends
and the people outside.

01:06:24.200 --> 01:06:27.613
It was always about my family,
you know, what they think.

01:06:29.200 --> 01:06:31.789
And if they didn't, I would
say who gives a fuck anyway,

01:06:31.789 --> 01:06:33.880
I'm not supposed to quit,
but just as long as,

01:06:33.880 --> 01:06:35.730
if I got that as something,
if I didn't, it was like,

01:06:35.730 --> 01:06:38.020
oh, well, I'm gonna still
do what I want to do anyway.

01:06:38.020 --> 01:06:40.650
But just to, I guess, just
to make it okay for me

01:06:40.650 --> 01:06:43.810
to not have to worry, that
was a little, one less thing

01:06:43.810 --> 01:06:46.690
I had to worry about is like
struggling with my family,

01:06:46.690 --> 01:06:48.723
with who I was, you know?

01:06:50.070 --> 01:06:51.960
And that was a blessing.

01:06:51.960 --> 01:06:55.280
My aunts and uncles and
cousins, they like secondary,

01:06:55.280 --> 01:06:58.333
you know, so, but they okay with it.

01:07:00.923 --> 01:07:02.620
What they say, they
said, "Hey, little cuz,"

01:07:02.620 --> 01:07:03.733
they just called me "little cuz."

01:07:03.733 --> 01:07:06.649
they don't even go to the name.

01:07:06.649 --> 01:07:07.730
My cousin's like, "Hey, little cuz,"

01:07:07.730 --> 01:07:10.483
we were laughing, like
whatever your name is,

01:07:10.483 --> 01:07:14.053
just you my cousin, so
that's how we relate.

01:07:16.070 --> 01:07:20.843
But the hardest one, the emotional,

01:07:21.820 --> 01:07:23.083
was my baby sister.

01:07:24.250 --> 01:07:25.130
That's my sister.

01:07:25.130 --> 01:07:26.070
That was my heart.

01:07:26.070 --> 01:07:29.400
That was not was, but she still is.

01:07:29.400 --> 01:07:31.940
She could not get it.

01:07:31.940 --> 01:07:34.510
She, she wasn't gonna get it.

01:07:34.510 --> 01:07:36.250
She told me I'm not gonna,

01:07:36.250 --> 01:07:39.480
she's the only one verbally expressed it.

01:07:39.480 --> 01:07:41.230
You know, my other, they was just like,

01:07:41.230 --> 01:07:43.807
they did ask, but my sis
said, "I don't understand it.

01:07:43.807 --> 01:07:45.690
"I don't like it, I don't get it."

01:07:45.690 --> 01:07:50.690
And we even went through the
point where she even cried,

01:07:51.590 --> 01:07:54.930
you know, I ain't know black
people would do that, either.

01:07:54.930 --> 01:07:58.080
But because of her background, she was,

01:07:58.080 --> 01:08:01.030
she was into more church
and she was into her,

01:08:01.030 --> 01:08:04.168
she was into a little bit
experience of life, too,

01:08:04.168 --> 01:08:07.743
to where she just, (sighing)

01:08:11.310 --> 01:08:14.430
because, and the reason being that I knew,

01:08:14.430 --> 01:08:16.350
and I knew she was gonna take the hardest,

01:08:16.350 --> 01:08:18.680
because we were the closest sibling.

01:08:18.680 --> 01:08:20.300
Even though I was close
to my other siblings,

01:08:20.300 --> 01:08:24.270
but we were more the closest
as far as sharing our feelings

01:08:24.270 --> 01:08:26.623
and what we're gonna be when we grow up.

01:08:33.280 --> 01:08:36.710
I know one day, when I first
moved back here, like I said,

01:08:36.710 --> 01:08:38.700
I went to each and I mean,
literally each and every

01:08:38.700 --> 01:08:41.260
one of my family homes that I'd thought,

01:08:41.260 --> 01:08:42.093
that I wanted to let know.

01:08:42.093 --> 01:08:45.280
And when I, when I told
my niece and nephew,

01:08:45.280 --> 01:08:47.887
which she did not like that, she said,

01:08:47.887 --> 01:08:48.897
"Why didn't you tell them that?

01:08:48.897 --> 01:08:51.127
"I don't want them to call you uncle.

01:08:51.127 --> 01:08:53.500
"I don't want them to do that."

01:08:53.500 --> 01:08:56.047
And I said, "Well, if we're
gonna hang out together

01:08:56.047 --> 01:08:57.887
"and when I'm going to
come over your house,

01:08:57.887 --> 01:09:01.247
"I'm not going to allow them
to call me anything else."

01:09:02.510 --> 01:09:05.467
I said, "You decide how
you, how you want them

01:09:05.467 --> 01:09:07.707
"to approach me, but
I'm not gonna allow them

01:09:07.707 --> 01:09:12.707
"to call me auntie," and my
nephew, he's like 16, 17 now.

01:09:13.350 --> 01:09:14.800
So he didn't, he don't say anything.

01:09:14.800 --> 01:09:16.580
He just like, you know,
if he wants something,

01:09:16.580 --> 01:09:20.683
he just asks, he doesn't call
me by any name or whatever.

01:09:24.100 --> 01:09:25.010
This is my sister.

01:09:25.010 --> 01:09:27.240
Like, since I've been
out of here, holidays,

01:09:27.240 --> 01:09:29.748
we, we spend together,
we talk on the phone,

01:09:29.748 --> 01:09:31.540
and I go over to her house for holidays

01:09:31.540 --> 01:09:33.620
and we do still play the games.

01:09:33.620 --> 01:09:35.870
We still play Monopoly and laugh 'til 2:00

01:09:35.870 --> 01:09:37.350
in the morning now today.

01:09:37.350 --> 01:09:40.140
And, you know, and play cards or whatever,

01:09:40.140 --> 01:09:42.600
you know, gin rummy,
she likes to play that.

01:09:42.600 --> 01:09:44.010
You know, we still do those things.

01:09:44.010 --> 01:09:45.874
So that part didn't go away.

01:09:45.874 --> 01:09:50.500
And I think the
misconception, just for people

01:09:50.500 --> 01:09:55.500
and family is that I'm
just gonna just be a total

01:09:56.910 --> 01:09:59.600
whatever the stereotype of a guy is.

01:09:59.600 --> 01:10:02.850
I'm just going to be this asshole,

01:10:02.850 --> 01:10:07.310
or I'm gonna be this aggressive person.

01:10:07.310 --> 01:10:10.403
And for me, that didn't never change.

01:10:11.800 --> 01:10:13.910
And my mom, she never treated me,

01:10:13.910 --> 01:10:15.750
well, she never forced me,

01:10:15.750 --> 01:10:17.280
you know, I hear a lot of stories where,

01:10:17.280 --> 01:10:19.710
you know, they were
forced to wear dresses,

01:10:19.710 --> 01:10:20.960
and forced to be girly.

01:10:20.960 --> 01:10:22.650
My mom, she was, it's like, you know,

01:10:22.650 --> 01:10:25.630
they say your mom knew,
you know, and just looking,

01:10:25.630 --> 01:10:27.713
I know my mom knew I was different.

01:10:28.758 --> 01:10:32.270
And so she allowed me to be different,

01:10:32.270 --> 01:10:36.330
you know, 'cause I always
spoke my mind and I say, no,

01:10:36.330 --> 01:10:38.880
I don't wanna, I say
yes, this is what I want.

01:10:38.880 --> 01:10:43.307
And so my mom allowed me to be who I was

01:10:43.307 --> 01:10:47.110
without, you know, being
disrespectful to adults.

01:10:47.110 --> 01:10:48.340
'Cause back then adults,

01:10:48.340 --> 01:10:50.703
they'd get in your butt
back then, you know?

01:10:52.089 --> 01:10:54.600
And when I knew, just growing up

01:10:54.600 --> 01:10:57.600
that my mom allowed me to say yes or no,

01:10:57.600 --> 01:10:59.440
or I don't want this,
or I don't like this,

01:10:59.440 --> 01:11:03.736
or I wanna go here, it helped me in life,

01:11:03.736 --> 01:11:06.393
that I don't have to be a
yes person all the time.

01:11:07.630 --> 01:11:09.170
Well, depending on the
type of relationship type,

01:11:09.170 --> 01:11:12.060
with the type of women I
was in, I was always, a yes,

01:11:12.060 --> 01:11:15.075
you know, yes, yes,
honey, yes, this and that.

01:11:15.075 --> 01:11:16.760
That that came later in life.

01:11:16.760 --> 01:11:19.180
My mom didn't tell me it
wasn't gonna be like that.

01:11:19.180 --> 01:11:21.560
Man, she didn't tell me about that part,

01:11:21.560 --> 01:11:24.443
but my brothers did, you're
talking about growing up.

01:11:25.890 --> 01:11:29.010
One of my biggest mentor was my brother.

01:11:29.010 --> 01:11:34.010
He passed away, in '03,
but he taught me the,

01:11:34.550 --> 01:11:38.290
he taught me the bad side of growing up.

01:11:39.310 --> 01:11:43.580
He taught me, you know, what women like,

01:11:43.580 --> 01:11:47.590
what women, what they
expect and how to treat them

01:11:47.590 --> 01:11:51.100
and what to do, when they say this

01:11:51.100 --> 01:11:53.690
and it wasn't the right thing to do,

01:11:53.690 --> 01:11:56.310
but it worked, it worked
up until, you know,

01:11:56.310 --> 01:11:58.910
I've met as I get older, I met a woman

01:11:58.910 --> 01:12:02.040
that knew, you know, it's
like, she knew my brother.

01:12:02.040 --> 01:12:03.073
She knew guys like my brother.

01:12:03.073 --> 01:12:05.700
She was like, that's now how it works.

01:12:05.700 --> 01:12:07.500
My mom taught me this, you know?

01:12:07.500 --> 01:12:11.953
And I always say, once I met my match,

01:12:13.210 --> 01:12:14.550
the last relationship I was in,

01:12:14.550 --> 01:12:18.072
I met my match and it was
like, I had to either change

01:12:18.072 --> 01:12:21.040
or I have to start dating younger girls

01:12:21.040 --> 01:12:22.590
that didn't know any different.

01:12:24.320 --> 01:12:28.998
So, but I always dated, women,

01:12:28.998 --> 01:12:31.293
like a mixture of my
mom and my two sisters.

01:12:33.850 --> 01:12:36.477
Because my sisters, they're beautiful,

01:12:36.477 --> 01:12:39.260
